window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert-math-continuity","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Continuity and Differentiability · Practice","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","subject":"Maths","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":1,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The function $f(x) = |x|$ is continuous at $x = 0$.\nStatement II: Every function that is continuous at a point is also differentiable at that point.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: For $|x|$ at 0, the left hand limit is $\\lim_{x\\to0^-}(-x)=0$. The right hand limit is $\\lim_{x\\to0^+}x=0$. Also $f(0)=0$. All three agree, so Statement I is true.\nStatement II: Differentiable implies continuous, but the converse fails.\nFor $|x|$ at 0, the left hand derivative is $-1$ and the right hand derivative is $1$. So $|x|$ is continuous but not differentiable at 0, and Statement II is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":2,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (function) with List-II (differential equation it satisfies), where $y' = \\frac{dy}{dx}$ and $y'' = \\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}$.\nList-I:\n(a) $y = 2e^{3x} - e^{-3x}$\n(b) $y = 4\\sin 3x + \\cos 3x$\n(c) $y = e^{x} + e^{2x}$\n(d) $y = x^3$\nList-II:\n(i) $y'' - 3y' + 2y = 0$\n(ii) $y'' = 9y$\n(iii) $xy'' = 2y'$\n(iv) $y'' = -9y$","image":"","options":["a-iv, b-ii, c-i, d-iii","a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii","a-ii, b-iv, c-iii, d-i","a-i, b-iv, c-ii, d-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Work out each one:\n(a) $y'' = 18e^{3x} - 9e^{-3x} = 9(2e^{3x} - e^{-3x}) = 9y$, so a-ii.\n(b) $y'' = -36\\sin 3x - 9\\cos 3x = -9y$, so b-iv.\n(c) $y' = e^x + 2e^{2x}$ and $y'' = e^x + 4e^{2x}$. Then $y'' - 3y' + 2y = (1 - 3 + 2)e^x + (4 - 6 + 2)e^{2x} = 0$, so c-i.\n(d) $y' = 3x^2$ and $y'' = 6x$, so $xy'' = 6x^2 = 2y'$, giving d-iii.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":3,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $f(x) = |x|^3$ for all real $x$, then $f''(x)$ equals:","image":"","options":["$6x$ for all real $x$","$6|x|$ for all real $x$","$3x^2$ for all real $x$","$6|x|$ for $x \\neq 0$, and $f''(0)$ does not exist"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Write $f(x) = x^3$ for $x \\ge 0$ and $f(x) = -x^3$ for $x < 0$.\nAway from 0: $f'(x) = 3x^2$ for $x > 0$ and $f'(x) = -3x^2$ for $x < 0$. At 0, $f'(0) = \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{|h|^3}{h} = 0$. So $f'(x) = 3x|x|$.\nDifferentiating again away from 0 gives $6x$ for $x > 0$ and $-6x$ for $x < 0$, i.e. $6|x|$.\nAt 0: $f''(0) = \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{3h|h|}{h} = \\lim_{h \\to 0} 3|h| = 0$, so $f''(0)$ exists.\nHence $f''(x) = 6|x|$ for all real $x$, which rules out option (4).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":4,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"The function $f$ is defined by $f(x)=\\frac{x^2-9}{x-3}$ for $x\\neq 3$ and $f(3)=k$. For what value of $k$ is $f$ continuous at $x=3$?","image":"","options":["0","3","6","9"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $f(x)=\\frac{x^2-9}{x-3}$ for $x\\neq3$ and $f(3)=k$.\nCondition: $f$ is continuous at 3 if $\\lim_{x\\to3}f(x)=f(3)$.\nFor $x\\neq3$: $\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)}{x-3}=x+3$, so the limit is $3+3=6$.\nSo $k=6$. Option (2) wrongly uses $x$ instead of $x+3$. Option (1) gives 0 by putting $x=3$ only in the numerator.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":5,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (functions, with x in a suitable domain) with List-II (derivatives).\nList-I:\n(A) $x^x$\n(B) $x^{1/x}$\n(C) $(\\sin x)^{\\sin x}$\n(D) $x^{\\log x}$\nList-II:\n(I) $2x^{\\log x - 1}\\log x$\n(II) $x^x(1+\\log x)$\n(III) $x^{1/x}\\cdot\\frac{1-\\log x}{x^2}$\n(IV) $(\\sin x)^{\\sin x}\\cos x\\,(1+\\log\\sin x)$","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $\\log y = x\\log x$, so $y' = x^x(1+\\log x)$, which is (II).\n(B) $\\log y = \\frac{\\log x}{x}$, so $\\frac{y'}{y} = \\frac{1-\\log x}{x^2}$, which is (III).\n(C) $\\log y = \\sin x\\log\\sin x$, so $\\frac{y'}{y} = \\cos x\\log\\sin x + \\cos x$, which is (IV).\n(D) $\\log y = (\\log x)^2$, so $\\frac{y'}{y} = \\frac{2\\log x}{x}$ and $y' = 2x^{\\log x-1}\\log x$, which is (I).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":6,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $y=\\tan(5x)$, then $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ equals","image":"","options":["$5\\sec(5x)\\tan(5x)$","$5\\sec^2(5x)$","$\\sec^2(5x)$","$\\frac{1}{5}\\sec^2(5x)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"From the derivative table, $\\frac{d}{dt}(\\tan t)=\\sec^2 t$.\nWith $t=5x$, we have $\\frac{dt}{dx}=5$.\nSo $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\sec^2(5x)\\cdot 5=5\\sec^2(5x)$.\nOption 1 uses the derivative of $\\sec$, not $\\tan$. Option 3 omits the inner derivative.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":7,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"For the parabola given by $x = 3t^2$, $y = 6t$, find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $(12, 12)$.","image":"","options":["$2$","$\\frac{1}{4}$","$\\frac{1}{2}$","$1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $x = 3t^2$, $y = 6t$. The point $(12, 12)$ gives $6t = 12$, so $t = 2$. This also satisfies $3t^2 = 12$.\nFormula: $\\frac{dx}{dt} = 6t$ and $\\frac{dy}{dt} = 6$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{6}{6t} = \\frac{1}{t}$.\nSubstituting $t = 2$: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{1}{2}$.\nThe value $2$ comes from inverting the ratio, i.e. computing $\\frac{dx}{dy}$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":8,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Match each function in List-I with the differential equation it satisfies in List-II ($y_1 = \\frac{dy}{dx}$, $y_2 = \\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}$).\nList-I:\n(a) $y = e^{2x} + e^{-2x}$\n(b) $y = \\sin 2x$\n(c) $y = e^{x} + e^{2x}$\n(d) $y = \\tan^{-1} x$\nList-II:\n(i) $y_2 + 4y = 0$\n(ii) $(1 + x^2)y_2 + 2xy_1 = 0$\n(iii) $y_2 = 4y$\n(iv) $y_2 - 3y_1 + 2y = 0$","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-i, b-iii, c-iv, d-ii","a-iii, b-i, c-ii, d-iv","a-iv, b-i, c-iii, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) $y_2=4e^{2x}+4e^{-2x}=4y$, so a-iii.\n(b) $y_2=-4\\sin2x=-4y$, so b-i.\n(c) $y_1=e^x+2e^{2x}$ and $y_2=e^x+4e^{2x}$. Then $y_2-3y_1+2y=(1-3+2)e^x+(4-6+2)e^{2x}=0$, so c-iv.\n(d) $(1+x^2)y_1=1$. Differentiating gives $(1+x^2)y_2+2xy_1=0$, so d-ii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":9,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Consider $f(x)=\\begin{cases} \\lambda(x^2-3x), & x\\le 0 \\\\ 2x+1, & x>0 \\end{cases}$, where $\\lambda$ is a real constant.\nStatement I: There is no real value of $\\lambda$ for which $f$ is continuous at $x=0$.\nStatement II: $f$ is continuous at $x=1$ for every real value of $\\lambda$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"At $x=0$: $f(0)$ and the left hand limit both equal $\\lambda(0-0)=0$ for every $\\lambda$.\nThe right hand limit is $\\lim_{x\\to0^+}(2x+1)=1$.\nSince $0\\ne1$ whatever $\\lambda$ is, no $\\lambda$ makes $f$ continuous at 0. Statement I is true.\nNear $x=1$, $f(x)=2x+1$, a polynomial. So $f$ is continuous at 1 regardless of $\\lambda$. Statement II is true.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":10,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $f(x)=\\frac{1-\\sin x}{(\\pi-2x)^2}$ for $x\\ne\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=k$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$, then $k$ equals","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{8}$","$\\frac{1}{4}$","$\\frac{1}{2}$","$0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Put $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+h$, with $h\\to 0$. Then $\\sin x=\\cos h$ and $(\\pi-2x)^2=4h^2$.\nThe numerator becomes $1-\\cos h=2\\sin^2\\frac{h}{2}$.\nSo $f=\\frac{2\\sin^2(h/2)}{4h^2}=\\frac{1}{8}\\left(\\frac{\\sin(h/2)}{h/2}\\right)^2\\to\\frac{1}{8}$.\nContinuity requires $k=$ limit $=\\frac{1}{8}$. Getting $\\frac{1}{2}$ comes from forgetting the factor 4 in the denominator.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":11,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $y = \\sin x\\cdot\\sin 2x\\cdot\\sin 3x$, then on an interval where each factor is positive, $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ equals:","image":"","options":["$y(\\cot x + 2\\cot 2x + 3\\cot 3x)$","$y(\\cot x + \\cot 2x + \\cot 3x)$","$6\\cos x\\cos 2x\\cos 3x$","$-y(\\tan x + 2\\tan 2x + 3\\tan 3x)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Take logs: $\\log y = \\log\\sin x + \\log\\sin 2x + \\log\\sin 3x$.\nDifferentiate each term with the chain rule: $\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x} + \\frac{2\\cos 2x}{\\sin 2x} + \\frac{3\\cos 3x}{\\sin 3x}$.\nSo $\\frac{dy}{dx} = y(\\cot x+2\\cot 2x+3\\cot 3x)$.\nOption (2) drops the chain-rule factors 2 and 3. Option (3) wrongly differentiates each factor separately and multiplies the results.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":12,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"The function $f(x) = x^2$ for $x \\le 1$ and $f(x) = ax + b$ for $x > 1$ is differentiable at $x = 1$. The value of $a - b$ is:","image":"","options":["$3$","$1$","$-3$","$2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $f$ is differentiable at $x = 1$, so it must also be continuous there.\nContinuity: $1^2 = a(1) + b$, so $a + b = 1$.\nEqual one-sided derivatives: LHD $= 2x$ at $x = 1$, which is 2, and RHD $= a$. So $a = 2$.\nSubstituting: $b = 1 - 2 = -1$.\nSo $a - b = 2 - (-1) = 3$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":13,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The function $f(x) = \\tan x$ is a continuous function.\nStatement II: The function $g(x) = \\frac{1}{x}$, $x \\ne 0$, is a continuous function.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A function is called continuous if it is continuous at every point of its domain.\nStatement I: $\\tan x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}$ is a quotient of continuous functions. It is continuous wherever it is defined, i.e. for $x \\ne (2n+1)\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. True.\nStatement II: For any $c \\ne 0$, $\\lim_{x\\to c}\\frac{1}{x} = \\frac{1}{c}$. The point 0 is not in the domain, so it does not count. True.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":14,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $y = (\\sin x)^{\\tan x}$ for $0 < x < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, then $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at $x = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$ equals","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1+\\log 2}{\\sqrt{2}}$","$\\sqrt{2}\\,(1-\\log 2)$","$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} - \\log 2$","$\\frac{1-\\log 2}{\\sqrt{2}}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Take logs: $\\log y = \\tan x \\, \\log(\\sin x)$. This is valid because $\\sin x > 0$ on $(0, \\frac{\\pi}{2})$.\nDifferentiate: $\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\sec^2 x \\, \\log(\\sin x) + \\tan x \\cdot \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x} = \\sec^2 x \\, \\log(\\sin x) + 1$.\nAt $x = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$: $\\sec^2 x = 2$ and $\\log\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = -\\frac{1}{2}\\log 2$, so $\\frac{y'}{y} = 1 - \\log 2$.\nAlso $y = \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)^1 = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$.\nSo $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{1-\\log 2}{\\sqrt{2}}$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":15,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following derivative statements are correct?\n(i) $\\frac{d}{dx}[\\sin(\\cos x)]=-\\sin x\\,\\cos(\\cos x)$\n(ii) $\\frac{d}{dx}[\\cos^2 x]=-\\sin 2x$\n(iii) $\\frac{d}{dx}[\\sec 3x]=3\\sec 3x\\tan 3x$\n(iv) $\\frac{d}{dx}[\\sin^3 x]=3\\sin^2 x$","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the outer derivative is $\\cos(\\cos x)$ and the inner derivative is $-\\sin x$.\n(ii) Correct: $2\\cos x\\cdot(-\\sin x)=-\\sin 2x$.\n(iii) Correct: $\\sec 3x\\tan 3x\\cdot 3$.\n(iv) Incorrect: $\\sin^3 x=(\\sin x)^3$, so its derivative is $3\\sin^2 x\\cos x$. The factor $\\cos x$ is missing.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":16,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following functions are continuous at every real number?\n(i) $x^3 + x^2 - 1$\n(ii) $\\sin(x^2)$\n(iii) $|1 - x + |x||$\n(iv) $[x]$, the greatest integer function\n(v) $e^x$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Continuous: every polynomial is continuous.\n(ii) Continuous: it is a composite of the continuous functions $\\sin t$ and $x^2$.\n(iii) Continuous: $1-x+|x|$ is a sum of continuous functions, and taking the modulus of a continuous function keeps it continuous.\n(iv) Not continuous: at each integer $c$, the left hand limit is $c-1$ and the right hand limit is $c$.\n(v) Continuous on all of R.\nSo 4 of the functions are continuous everywhere.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":17,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Find $k$ so that $f(x)=\\begin{cases} kx+2, & x\\le \\pi \\\\ \\cos x, & x>\\pi \\end{cases}$ is continuous at $x=\\pi$.","image":"","options":["$-\\frac{3}{\\pi}$","$\\frac{3}{\\pi}$","$-\\frac{1}{\\pi}$","$-\\frac{2}{\\pi}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $f(\\pi)=k\\pi+2$ and RHL $=\\lim_{x\\to\\pi^+}\\cos x=\\cos\\pi=-1$.\nCondition: $k\\pi+2=-1$.\nSo $k\\pi=-3$, which gives $k=-\\frac{3}{\\pi}$.\nTaking $\\cos\\pi=+1$ would give $-\\frac{1}{\\pi}$, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":18,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: $\\frac{d}{dx}\\left[\\sin^2(x^5)\\right]=10x^4\\sin(x^5)\\cos(x^5)$\nStatement II: $\\frac{d}{dx}\\left[\\cos(x^3)\\cdot\\sin^2(x^5)\\right]=-3x^2\\sin(x^3)\\cdot 10x^4\\sin(x^5)\\cos(x^5)$\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: $\\sin^2(x^5)=[\\sin(x^5)]^2$ is a composite of three functions. Its derivative is $2\\sin(x^5)\\cdot\\cos(x^5)\\cdot 5x^4=10x^4\\sin(x^5)\\cos(x^5)$. Correct.\nStatement II: this is a product, so the product rule $(uv)'=u'v+uv'$ is needed, not the product of the two derivatives.\nThe true derivative is $-3x^2\\sin(x^3)\\sin^2(x^5)+10x^4\\cos(x^3)\\sin(x^5)\\cos(x^5)$. So Statement II is incorrect.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":19,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"For the circle $(x - 2)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 9$, find the value of $\\dfrac{\\left[1 + \\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)^2\\right]^{3/2}}{\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}}$ at the point $(3.8, 1.4)$ on the circle.","image":"","options":["$-3$","$3$","$9$","$-\\frac{1}{3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Differentiating implicitly: $y' = -\\frac{x-2}{y+1}$.\nDifferentiating again: $y'' = -\\frac{(y+1) - (x-2)y'}{(y+1)^2} = -\\frac{(y+1)^2 + (x-2)^2}{(y+1)^3} = -\\frac{9}{(y+1)^3}$.\nAt the point, $x - 2 = 1.8$ and $y + 1 = 2.4$. So $y' = -0.75$ and $1 + y'^2 = 1.5625$, giving $(1.5625)^{3/2} = 1.953125$.\nAlso $y'' = -\\frac{9}{13.824}$.\nRatio $= 1.953125 \\times \\left(-\\frac{13.824}{9}\\right) = -3$. Its magnitude equals the radius, independent of the centre.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":20,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (functions) with List-II (behaviour at x = 0):\nList-I\n(a) $f(x)=1$ for $x\\le0$, $f(x)=2$ for $x>0$\n(b) $f(x)=1$ for $x\\ne0$, $f(x)=2$ for $x=0$\n(c) $f(x)=\\frac{\\sin x}{x}$ for $x<0$, $f(x)=x+1$ for $x\\ge0$\n(d) $f(x)=x^2\\sin\\frac{1}{x}$ for $x\\ne0$, $f(0)=0$\nList-II\n(i) LHL ≠ RHL\n(ii) LHL = RHL ≠ f(0)\n(iii) continuous at 0, common limit equal to 1\n(iv) continuous at 0, common limit equal to 0","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) LHL = 1 and RHL = 2. The limits differ, so (a) matches (i).\n(b) LHL = RHL = 1 but f(0) = 2, so (b) matches (ii).\n(c) $\\lim_{x\\to0^-}\\frac{\\sin x}{x}=1$, RHL = 0 + 1 = 1 and f(0) = 1. It is continuous with limit 1, so (c) matches (iii).\n(d) Since $|x^2\\sin\\frac1x|\\le x^2\\to0$, the limit is 0 = f(0). So (d) matches (iv).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":21,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The function $f(x) = \\sin(x^2)$ is continuous at every real number.\nReason (R): If h is continuous at c and g is continuous at h(c), then $g \\circ h$ is continuous at c.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: it is exactly Theorem 2 on composite functions.\nWrite $f = g \\circ h$ with $h(x) = x^2$ (a polynomial, so continuous everywhere) and $g(x) = \\sin x$ (continuous everywhere).\nFor any real c, h is continuous at c and g is continuous at $h(c) = c^2$.\nSo f is continuous at every real number. A is true, and R is the reason.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":22,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"The function\n$f(x)=3$ if $x\\le 1$; $f(x)=ax+b$ if $1<x<4$; $f(x)=12$ if $x\\ge 4$\nis continuous on $\\mathbb{R}$. Find the value of $a-b$.","image":"","options":["3","-3","9","0"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"At $x=1$: LHL $=3=f(1)$ and RHL $=a+b$. So $a+b=3$.\nAt $x=4$: LHL $=4a+b$ and RHL $=12=f(4)$. So $4a+b=12$.\nSubtracting the equations: $3a=9$, so $a=3$ and $b=0$.\nSo $a-b=3$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":23,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For $0<x<1$, $\\frac{d}{dx}\\left[\\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1-x^2}{1+x^2}\\right)\\right]=\\frac{2}{1+x^2}$.\nReason (R): Putting $x=\\tan\\theta$ gives $\\frac{1-x^2}{1+x^2}=\\cos2\\theta$, and for $0<x<1$ the angle $2\\theta$ lies in $\\left(0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$, so $\\cos^{-1}(\\cos2\\theta)=2\\theta$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R: With $x=\\tan\\theta$, $\\frac{1-\\tan^2\\theta}{1+\\tan^2\\theta}=\\cos2\\theta$.\nFor $0<x<1$, $\\theta\\in\\left(0,\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$, so $2\\theta\\in\\left(0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$, which lies within the principal range $[0,\\pi]$. So R is true.\nA: The expression simplifies to $2\\theta=2\\tan^{-1}x$, whose derivative is $\\frac{2}{1+x^2}$. So A is true.\nThis simplification is exactly the argument that establishes A, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":24,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"For the curve $x^2 - xy + y^2 = 3$, consider the following statements:\n(i) $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{y - 2x}{2y - x}$\n(ii) At (1, 2), $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0$\n(iii) At (2, 1), the expression for $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ is not defined\n(iv) At (1, −1), $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -1$\nHow many of these statements are correct?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Differentiate implicitly: $2x - y - x\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0$.\n(i) Correct: rearranging gives $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{y - 2x}{2y - x}$.\n(ii) Correct: (1, 2) lies on the curve since $1 - 2 + 4 = 3$, and $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2 - 2}{4 - 1} = 0$.\n(iii) Correct: (2, 1) lies on the curve since $4 - 2 + 1 = 3$, and the denominator is $2 - 2 = 0$.\n(iv) Incorrect: (1, −1) lies on the curve since $1 + 1 + 1 = 3$, but $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{-1 - 2}{-2 - 1} = 1$, not −1.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":25,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Let $b > 1$. Which of the following statements about the logarithmic function $y = \\log_b x$ is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Its domain is the set of all positive real numbers, $\\mathbf{R}^+$.","Its graph always passes through the point $(1, 0)$.","As $x$ approaches $0$ from the right, its graph approaches the y-axis in the second quadrant.","The graph of $y = \\log_e x$ is the mirror image of the graph of $y = e^x$ in the line $y = x$."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the logarithm of a non-positive number has no meaningful definition, so the domain is $\\mathbf{R}^+$.\n(2) Correct: $b^0 = 1$, so $\\log_b 1 = 0$ and the graph passes through $(1, 0)$.\n(3) Incorrect: for $x$ near $0$ (and $x > 0$), $\\log_b x$ becomes very large and negative. So the graph approaches the y-axis in the fourth quadrant, not the second. It is the exponential graph that approaches the x-axis in the second quadrant.\n(4) Correct: $e^x$ and $\\ln x$ are inverses, so their graphs are reflections of each other in $y = x$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":26,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x)=\\frac{k\\cos x}{\\pi-2x}$ for $x\\ne \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=5$.\nStatement I: If $f$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$, then $k=10$.\nStatement II: $\\lim_{x\\to \\pi/2}\\frac{\\cos x}{\\pi-2x}=-\\frac{1}{2}$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Put $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+h$. Then $\\cos x=-\\sin h$ and $\\pi-2x=-2h$.\nSo $\\frac{\\cos x}{\\pi-2x}=\\frac{\\sin h}{2h}\\to \\frac{1}{2}$. The limit is positive, so Statement II is incorrect.\nContinuity requires $\\frac{k}{2}=5$, which gives $k=10$. Statement I is correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":27,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If $y = 5e^{3x} - 2e^{-3x}$, then $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = 9y$.\nStatement II: If $y = Ae^{mx} + Be^{nx}$, then $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} - (m+n)\\frac{dy}{dx} + mny = 0$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: $y' = 15e^{3x} + 6e^{-3x}$ and $y'' = 45e^{3x} - 18e^{-3x} = 9(5e^{3x} - 2e^{-3x}) = 9y$. True.\nStatement II: $y' = mAe^{mx} + nBe^{nx}$ and $y'' = m^2Ae^{mx} + n^2Be^{nx}$.\nThe coefficient of $Ae^{mx}$ in $y'' - (m+n)y' + mny$ is $m^2 - m^2 - mn + mn = 0$, and likewise for $Be^{nx}$. True.\nIn fact, Statement I is the case $m = 3$, $n = -3$ of Statement II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":28,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x)=\\begin{cases} \\frac{k\\cos x}{2x-\\pi}, & x<\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\\\ 3, & x=\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\\\ m\\sin x, & x>\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\end{cases}$\nIf $f$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$, then $k+m$ equals","image":"","options":["$9$","$3$","$-3$","$-9$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Put $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+h$. Then $\\cos x=-\\sin h$ and $2x-\\pi=2h$.\nLeft hand limit: $\\lim_{h\\to0^-}\\frac{-k\\sin h}{2h}=-\\frac{k}{2}$.\nSetting $-\\frac{k}{2}=f(\\frac{\\pi}{2})=3$ gives $k=-6$.\nRight hand limit: $m\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{2}=m$. Setting $m=3$.\nSo $k+m=-6+3=-3$. The option 9 is the trap: it comes from dropping the sign and taking $k=6$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":29,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"For $f(x) = |1 - x + |x||$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, which of the following is correct?","image":"","options":["$f(x) = 1$ for $x \\ge 0$, $f(x) = 1 - 2x$ for $x < 0$, and f is continuous everywhere","$f(x) = 1$ for $x \\ge 0$, $f(x) = 1 - 2x$ for $x < 0$, and f is discontinuous at x = 0","$f(x) = 1 - 2x$ for $x \\ge 0$, $f(x) = 1$ for $x < 0$, and f is continuous everywhere","$f(x) = |1 - 2x|$ for all x, so f is discontinuous at $x = \\frac{1}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For $x \\ge 0$: $|x| = x$, so $f(x) = |1 - x + x| = 1$.\nFor $x < 0$: $|x| = -x$, so $f(x) = |1 - 2x| = 1 - 2x$, since $1 - 2x > 0$ here.\nAt x = 0: the left-hand limit is $1 - 0 = 1$, and the right-hand limit and f(0) both equal 1. So f is continuous at 0.\nThis agrees with f being the modulus of the continuous function $1 - x + |x|$, which makes f continuous everywhere.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":30,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $f(x) = \\tan^{-1}(x^2)$, then $f'(1)$ equals:","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{2}$","$1$","$2$","$\\frac{1}{4}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $f(x)=\\tan^{-1}(x^2)$.\nFormula: $\\frac{d}{dx}\\tan^{-1}t=\\frac{1}{1+t^2}$, and by the chain rule $f'(x)=\\frac{1}{1+x^4}\\cdot 2x$.\nSubstituting $x=1$: $f'(1)=\\frac{2}{1+1}=1$.\nThe value $\\frac{1}{2}$ comes from forgetting the factor $2x$ from the chain rule.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":31,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"A curve is given by $x = a(\\theta - \\sin\\theta)$, $y = a(1 - \\cos\\theta)$ with $a = 2$. Find $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}$ at $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.","image":"","options":["$-0.5$","$0.5$","$-0.25$","$-1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Parametric derivatives: $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta} = a(1 - \\cos\\theta)$ and $\\frac{dy}{d\\theta} = a\\sin\\theta$.\nFirst derivative: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{\\sin\\theta}{1 - \\cos\\theta} = \\cot\\frac{\\theta}{2}$.\nSecond derivative: $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = -\\frac{1}{2}\\csc^2\\frac{\\theta}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{a(1-\\cos\\theta)} = -\\frac{1}{2}\\csc^2\\frac{\\theta}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2a\\sin^2\\frac{\\theta}{2}} = -\\frac{1}{4a}\\csc^4\\frac{\\theta}{2}$.\nSubstituting $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$: $\\csc^4\\frac{\\pi}{4} = 4$, so the value is $-\\frac{4}{8} = -0.5$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":32,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (function) with List-II (derivative w.r.t. $x$):\nList-I\n(A) $\\sin(x^2)$\n(B) $\\cos(\\sqrt{x})$, $x>0$\n(C) $\\tan(2x+1)$\n(D) $(x^2+1)^3$\nList-II\n(i) $6x(x^2+1)^2$\n(ii) $2x\\cos(x^2)$\n(iii) $2\\sec^2(2x+1)$\n(iv) $-\\frac{\\sin\\sqrt{x}}{2\\sqrt{x}}$","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-iv, C-iii, D-i","A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i","A-i, B-iv, C-iii, D-ii","A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $\\cos(x^2)\\cdot 2x=2x\\cos(x^2)$, which is (ii).\n(B) $-\\sin\\sqrt{x}\\cdot\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}$, which is (iv).\n(C) $\\sec^2(2x+1)\\cdot 2$, which is (iii).\n(D) $3(x^2+1)^2\\cdot 2x=6x(x^2+1)^2$, which is (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":33,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Consider $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$, $x\\neq 0$.\nAssertion (A): The right hand limit of $f$ at $x=0$ does not exist as a real number.\nReason (R): As $x\\to0^+$, $f(x)$ can be made larger than any given number, and $+\\infty$ is not a real number.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. At $x=10^{-n}$ we get $f(x)=10^n$, which grows without bound. We write $\\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\frac1x=+\\infty$.\nNCERT stresses that $+\\infty$ is NOT a real number.\nA is true. A limit equal to $+\\infty$ means no real limit exists.\nThe unbounded growth in R is exactly why A holds, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":34,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"The point (2, 4) lies on the curve $x^y = y^x$ (x, y > 0). The value of $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at this point is approximately (take $\\log 2 = 0.693$):","image":"","options":["$-3.18$","$3.18$","$-0.31$","$2.00$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Take logs: $y\\log x = x\\log y$.\nDifferentiate: $y'\\log x + \\frac{y}{x} = \\log y + \\frac{x}{y}y'$.\nRearrange: $y'\\left(\\log x - \\frac{x}{y}\\right) = \\log y - \\frac{y}{x}$.\nSubstitute (2, 4): $y'(0.693-0.5) = 1.386-2$.\nSo $y' = \\frac{-0.614}{0.193} \\approx -3.18$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":35,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The greatest integer function $f(x) = [x]$ is discontinuous at every integer.\nStatement II: The domain of the logarithmic function $\\log_b x$ ($b > 1$) is the set of all real numbers.\n\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. For an integer $c$, $\\lim_{x\\to c^-}[x] = c-1$ and $\\lim_{x\\to c^+}[x] = c$. These one-sided limits are unequal, so $[x]$ is discontinuous at every integer.\nStatement II is false. The logarithm of a non-positive number cannot be defined, so the domain of the log function is $\\mathbb{R}^+$. It is the range of the log function that is all of $\\mathbb{R}$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":36,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"A curve is given parametrically by $x = at^2$, $y = 2at$ ($a \\neq 0$, $t \\neq 0$). Then $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}$ equals","image":"","options":["$-\\frac{1}{t^2}$","$\\frac{1}{2at^3}$","$-\\frac{1}{2at^3}$","$-\\frac{1}{at^2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1: $\\frac{dx}{dt} = 2at$ and $\\frac{dy}{dt} = 2a$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2a}{2at} = \\frac{1}{t}$.\nStep 2: $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = \\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right) = \\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\cdot\\frac{dt}{dx}$.\nStep 3: This gives $-\\frac{1}{t^2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{2at} = -\\frac{1}{2at^3}$.\nOption (1) is wrong because it differentiates $\\frac{1}{t}$ with respect to $t$ only. It forgets the factor $\\frac{dt}{dx}$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":37,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"For $x\\in(-1,1)$, $\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\sin^{-1}x+\\cos^{-1}x\\right)$ equals:","image":"","options":["$\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{2}$","$1$","$0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Formula: $\\frac{d}{dx}\\sin^{-1}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$ and $\\frac{d}{dx}\\cos^{-1}x=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$.\nAdding the two derivatives gives $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}=0$.\nThis agrees with the identity $\\sin^{-1}x+\\cos^{-1}x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$, which is a constant.\nThe option $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ is the value of the sum, not its derivative.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":38,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If $3x + 4y = \\cos y$, then $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{3}{4 + \\sin y}$.\nStatement II: $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos y) = -\\sin y$, where y is a function of x.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: Differentiate implicitly to get $3 + 4\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\sin y \\frac{dy}{dx}$. Then $\\frac{dy}{dx}(4 + \\sin y) = -3$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{3}{4 + \\sin y}$. Correct.\nStatement II: Since y depends on x, the chain rule gives $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos y) = -\\sin y \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}$. The factor $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ is missing, so the statement is incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":39,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"For what value of $k$ is the function $f(x)=\\begin{cases} kx^2, & x\\le 3 \\\\ 18, & x>3 \\end{cases}$ continuous at $x=3$?","image":"","options":["2","6","3","$\\frac{1}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $f(3)=k(3)^2=9k$ and RHL $=\\lim_{x\\to 3^+}18=18$.\nCondition: LHL = RHL = $f(3)$.\nSubstituting: $9k=18$.\nSo $k=2$. Writing $3k=18$ (which gives 6) forgets to square the 3.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":40,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $f(x) = (3x+2)^4$, then the value of $f'(0)$ is","image":"","options":["96","32","24","216"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $f(x) = (3x+2)^4$, a composite of $t = 3x+2$ and $v(t) = t^4$.\nChain rule: $f'(x) = \\frac{dv}{dt}\\cdot\\frac{dt}{dx} = 4t^3 \\cdot 3$.\nSo $f'(x) = 12(3x+2)^3$.\nSubstituting $x = 0$: $f'(0) = 12 \\times 2^3 = 12 \\times 8 = 96$.\nThe option 32 comes from $4 \\times 2^3$, which forgets the inner derivative 3.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":41,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"What is the derivative of $\\log(\\sec x)$ with respect to $\\tan x$ at $x = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$?","image":"","options":["$1$","$\\frac{1}{2}$","$2$","$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $u = \\log(\\sec x)$ and $v = \\tan x$.\nFormula: $\\frac{du}{dv} = \\frac{du/dx}{dv/dx}$.\nWorking: $\\frac{du}{dx} = \\frac{\\sec x\\tan x}{\\sec x} = \\tan x$ and $\\frac{dv}{dx} = \\sec^2 x$.\nSo $\\frac{du}{dv} = \\frac{\\tan x}{\\sec^2 x} = \\sin x\\cos x$.\nSubstituting $x = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$: $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{1}{2}$.\nThe value $1$ comes from taking only $\\frac{du}{dx}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":42,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"The function $f(x) = kx + 3$ for $x \\le 2$ and $f(x) = x^2 + 5$ for $x > 2$ is continuous at $x = 2$. Find $k$.","image":"","options":["3","2","6","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the function is continuous at $x = 2$, so LHL = RHL = $f(2)$.\nLHL $= f(2) = 2k + 3$.\nRHL $= \\lim_{x\\to 2^+}(x^2 + 5) = 9$.\nSetting them equal: $2k + 3 = 9$, so $2k = 6$.\nSo $k = 3$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":43,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"For the exponential function $y = b^x$ with $b > 1$, how many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Its domain is the set of all real numbers.\n(b) Its range is the set of all positive real numbers.\n(c) Its graph always passes through $(0, 1)$.\n(d) Its graph falls as we move from left to right.\n(e) For very large negative $x$, the graph meets the x-axis.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: the domain is R.\n(b) Correct: the range is all positive reals.\n(c) Correct: $b^0 = 1$, so $(0,1)$ is on the graph.\n(d) Incorrect: the function is ever increasing, so the graph rises.\n(e) Incorrect: the graph approaches the x-axis but never meets it.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":44,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The function $f(x) = |x|$ is continuous at $x = 0$.\nReason (R): Every function that is continuous at a point is also differentiable at that point.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A: For $|x|$ at 0, the LHL is $\\lim (-x) = 0$, the RHL is $\\lim x = 0$ and $f(0) = 0$. All three agree, so A is true.\nR: Differentiability implies continuity, but the converse fails. For $|x|$ at 0, the left derivative is $-1$ and the right derivative is $+1$, so $|x|$ is continuous there but not differentiable. R is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":45,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x) = \\lambda x^2 + 2$ for $x \\le 1$ and $f(x) = 4x - \\lambda$ for $x > 1$.\nStatement I: $f$ is continuous at $x = 1$ when $\\lambda = 1$.\nStatement II: For $\\lambda = 1$, $f$ is differentiable at $x = 1$.\n\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Continuity at 1: the left hand limit is $\\lambda + 2$ (which also equals $f(1)$), and the right hand limit is $4 - \\lambda$.\nSetting $\\lambda + 2 = 4 - \\lambda$ gives $\\lambda = 1$. So Statement I is true.\nDifferentiability with $\\lambda = 1$: the left piece $x^2 + 2$ has slope $2x = 2$ at $x = 1$, and the right piece $4x - 1$ has slope $4$.\nSince $2 \\neq 4$, $f$ is not differentiable at 1. So Statement II is false. Continuity does not guarantee differentiability.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":46,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $y = x^{2x}$, $x > 0$, then the value of $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at $x = 1$ is","image":"","options":["0","1","2","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Take logs: $\\log y = 2x \\log x$.\nDifferentiate: $\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{dy}{dx} = 2\\log x + 2x \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} = 2\\log x + 2$.\nSo $\\frac{dy}{dx} = x^{2x}(2\\log x + 2)$.\nAt $x = 1$: $y = 1^2 = 1$ and $\\log 1 = 0$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 1 \\times (0 + 2) = 2$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":47,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $y = x^3 + \\tan x$, then the value of $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}$ at $x = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$ is:","image":"","options":["$\\frac{3\\pi}{2} + 4$","$\\frac{3\\pi}{2} + 2$","$\\frac{3\\pi}{4} + 4$","$\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $y = x^3 + \\tan x$.\nFirst derivative: $y' = 3x^2 + \\sec^2 x$.\nSecond derivative: $y'' = 6x + 2\\sec x \\cdot \\sec x \\tan x = 6x + 2\\sec^2 x \\tan x$.\nSubstituting $x = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$: $\\sec^2 x = 2$ and $\\tan x = 1$, so $y'' = \\frac{6\\pi}{4} + 2(2)(1) = \\frac{3\\pi}{2} + 4$.\nOption (2) comes from forgetting the factor 2 that appears when $\\sec^2 x$ is differentiated.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":48,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (relation) with List-II ($\\frac{dy}{dx}$, wherever defined):\nList-I\n(A) $x - y = \\pi$\n(B) $x^2 + y^2 = 25$\n(C) $xy = 4$\n(D) $y^2 = 4x$\nList-II\n(1) $-\\frac{y}{x}$\n(2) $1$\n(3) $\\frac{2}{y}$\n(4) $-\\frac{x}{y}$","image":"","options":["A-2, B-1, C-4, D-3","A-2, B-4, C-1, D-3","A-1, B-4, C-2, D-3","A-2, B-4, C-3, D-1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) Differentiating gives $1 - \\frac{dy}{dx} = 0$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 1$. A matches 2.\n(B) Differentiating gives $2x + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{x}{y}$. B matches 4.\n(C) Differentiating gives $y + x\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{y}{x}$. C matches 1.\n(D) Differentiating gives $2y\\frac{dy}{dx} = 4$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2}{y}$. D matches 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":49,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"For the curve $x = 2(\\theta + \\sin\\theta)$, $y = 2(1 - \\cos\\theta)$, find $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}$ at $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.","image":"","options":["$1$","$0.25$","$2$","$0.5$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $a = 2$, and $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\tan\\frac{\\theta}{2}$.\nFormula: $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = \\frac{d}{d\\theta}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right) \\div \\frac{dx}{d\\theta}$.\nWorking: $\\frac{d}{d\\theta}\\left(\\tan\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1}{2}\\sec^2\\frac{\\theta}{2}$ and $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta} = a(1 + \\cos\\theta) = 2a\\cos^2\\frac{\\theta}{2}$.\nSo $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = \\frac{\\sec^4(\\theta/2)}{4a}$.\nSubstituting $a = 2$, $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$: $\\sec^4\\frac{\\pi}{4} = 4$, giving $\\frac{4}{8} = 0.5$.\nThe value $1$ comes from forgetting to divide by $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta}$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":50,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $f(x) = |x|^3$ for all real $x$, then $f''(x)$ for all real $x$ is:","image":"","options":["$6x$","$6|x|$","$3x|x|$","$6x|x|$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Write $f(x) = x^3$ for $x \\ge 0$ and $f(x) = -x^3$ for $x<0$.\nThis gives $f'(x) = 3x^2$ for $x>0$ and $f'(x) = -3x^2$ for $x<0$. At 0, $f'(0) = \\lim \\frac{|h|^3}{h} = 0$. Together, $f'(x) = 3x|x|$.\nDifferentiating again: $f''(x) = 6x$ for $x>0$ and $f''(x) = -6x$ for $x<0$.\nAt 0: $\\frac{f'(h) - f'(0)}{h} = 3|h| \\to 0$, so $f''(0) = 0$.\nHence $f''(x) = 6|x|$ for all x. Option (1) is wrong for negative x, and option (3) is $f'(x)$, not $f''(x)$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":51,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If $f + g$ is continuous at $x = c$, then both f and g must be continuous at $x = c$.\nStatement II: If f is continuous at $x = c$ and $f + g$ is continuous at $x = c$, then g is continuous at $x = c$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I: take $f(x) = [x]$ and $g(x) = -[x]$. Then $f + g = 0$ is continuous at x = 1, but neither f nor g is continuous there. So it is false.\nStatement II: write $g = (f + g) - f$. This is a difference of two functions continuous at c, so by Theorem 1, g is continuous at c. So it is true.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":52,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The function $\\sin^{-1}x$ is not differentiable at $x=1$, because $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$ is not defined there.\nStatement II: The derivative of $\\tan^{-1}x$ exists only for $x\\in(-1,1)$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: In deriving $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{1}{\\cos y}$ for $y=\\sin^{-1}x$, we need $\\cos y\\ne0$. That requires $x\\ne\\pm1$, so the formula holds only on $(-1,1)$. Correct.\nStatement II: $\\frac{d}{dx}\\tan^{-1}x=\\frac{1}{1+x^2}$, and $1+x^2$ is never zero. So the derivative exists for all real $x$, as in NCERT's table, where the domain is R. Incorrect.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":53,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"The function $f(x)=\\begin{cases} ax+2, & x\\le 2 \\\\ bx+6, & x>2 \\end{cases}$ is continuous at $x=2$. Which relation between $a$ and $b$ must hold?","image":"","options":["$a-b=2$","$b-a=2$","$a-b=4$","$a+b=2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For continuity at $x=2$: LHL = RHL = $f(2)$.\nLHL $= f(2) = 2a+2$, since $x=2$ falls in the first piece.\nRHL $= \\lim_{x\\to 2^+}(bx+6) = 2b+6$.\nEquating: $2a+2=2b+6$, so $2a-2b=4$, i.e. $a-b=2$.\nOption (2) reverses the sign and option (3) forgets to divide by 2.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":54,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"The derivative of $\\cos(x^3)$ with respect to $x$ is","image":"","options":["$3x^2\\sin(x^3)$","$-\\sin(3x^2)$","$-3x^2\\sin(x^3)$","$-3x^2\\cos(x^3)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Put $t=x^3$, so $y=\\cos t$.\nThen $\\frac{dy}{dt}=-\\sin t$ and $\\frac{dt}{dx}=3x^2$.\nChain rule: $\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\sin t\\cdot 3x^2=-3x^2\\sin(x^3)$.\nOption 1 has the wrong sign, since the derivative of $\\cos$ is $-\\sin$. Option 2 wrongly differentiates the inner function inside the sine instead of multiplying by its derivative.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":55,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x)=[x]+[-x]$, where $[\\cdot]$ is the greatest integer function.\nStatement I: $f$ is discontinuous at every integer.\nStatement II: At every integer $c$, $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exists.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"If $x$ is an integer, $[x]+[-x]=x-x=0$.\nIf $x$ is not an integer, $[-x]=-[x]-1$, so $f(x)=-1$.\nAt an integer $c$: LHL = RHL $=-1$, so the limit exists. Statement II is true.\nBut $f(c)=0\\neq-1$, so $f$ is discontinuous at each integer. Statement I is true.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":56,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (function) with List-II (derivative in its appropriate domain).\nList-I:\n(A) $\\sin^{-1} x$\n(B) $\\cos^{-1} x$\n(C) $\\tan^{-1} x$\n(D) $a^x$, $a > 0$\nList-II:\n(I) $\\frac{1}{1+x^2}$\n(II) $a^x \\log a$\n(III) $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$\n(IV) $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) For $y = \\sin^{-1}x$: $x = \\sin y$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{1}{\\cos y} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$. This is (III).\n(B) $\\cos^{-1}x$ has the negative of this derivative, $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$. This is (IV).\n(C) $\\tan^{-1}x$ gives $\\frac{1}{1+x^2}$. This is (I).\n(D) Write $a^x = e^{x\\log a}$, whose derivative is $a^x \\log a$. This is (II).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":57,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["A real function whose domain is a singleton set is continuous","For $f$ defined on $[a,b]$, continuity at $a$ requires only $\\lim_{x\\to a^+}f(x)=f(a)$","$f(x)=|x|$ is continuous at every real number","Every rational function $\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}$ is continuous at every real number"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT notes that a function defined at only one point is continuous there.\n(2) Correct: a left limit at $a$ makes no sense on $[a,b]$, so only the right hand limit is used.\n(3) Correct: $|x|$ is continuous for $c<0$, for $c>0$ and at 0, where LHL = RHL = 0 = $f(0)$.\n(4) Incorrect: a rational function is not defined where $q(x)=0$. It is continuous only on its domain.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":58,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If $x = t^2$ and $y = t^3$ ($t \\neq 0$), then $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2}{3}t$.\nStatement II: If $x^2 + xy + y^2 = 7$, then $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{2x + y}{x + 2y}$, wherever $x + 2y \\neq 0$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I: $\\frac{dx}{dt}=2t$ and $\\frac{dy}{dt}=3t^2$.\nSo $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3t^2}{2t}=\\frac{3t}{2}$, not $\\frac{2t}{3}$. Statement I is false.\nStatement II: Differentiate implicitly to get $2x+(y+x\\,y')+2y\\,y'=0$.\nThis gives $y'(x+2y)=-(2x+y)$, so $y'=-\\frac{2x+y}{x+2y}$. Statement II is true.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":59,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $x^y = e^{2(x-y)}$ for $x>0$, then $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ equals:","image":"","options":["$\\frac{2(1+\\log x)}{(2+\\log x)^2}$","$\\frac{2\\log x}{(2+\\log x)^2}$","$\\frac{1+\\log x}{2+\\log x}$","$\\frac{2}{(2+\\log x)^2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Take logs: $y\\log x = 2x-2y$.\nCollect the y terms: $y(2+\\log x) = 2x$, so $y = \\frac{2x}{2+\\log x}$.\nApply the quotient rule: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2(2+\\log x) - 2x\\cdot\\frac1x}{(2+\\log x)^2}$.\nThe numerator is $4+2\\log x-2 = 2+2\\log x$.\nSo $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2(1+\\log x)}{(2+\\log x)^2}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":60,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $f(x) = \\log_7(\\log x)$ for $x > 1$, where $\\log$ denotes the natural logarithm, then $f'(e)$ equals","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{e\\log 7}$","$\\frac{1}{\\log 7}$","$\\frac{7}{e}$","$\\frac{\\log 7}{e}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1 (change of base): $f(x) = \\frac{\\log(\\log x)}{\\log 7}$. Here $\\log 7$ is a constant.\nStep 2 (chain rule): $f'(x) = \\frac{1}{\\log 7}\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\log x}\\cdot\\frac{d}{dx}(\\log x) = \\frac{1}{x\\,\\log 7\\,\\log x}$.\nStep 3 (substitute $x = e$): $\\log e = 1$, so $f'(e) = \\frac{1}{e\\log 7}$.\nOption (2) forgets the factor $\\frac{1}{x}$ that comes from differentiating $\\log x$. Option (4) wrongly treats the base as a multiplier instead of dividing by $\\log 7$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":61,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) $f(x) = |x-1| + |x-2|$ is continuous everywhere but not differentiable at exactly two points.\n(ii) $g(x) = x - [x]$ is continuous at $x = 2.5$ but discontinuous at $x = 2$.\n(iii) $h(x) = \\sin|x|$ is differentiable at $x = 0$.\n(iv) If a function is continuous at $x = c$, it must be differentiable at $x = c$.\n(v) $x = e^{\\log x}$ holds for every real number $x$.\n(vi) $f(x) = x^2\\sin\\frac{1}{x}$ for $x \\neq 0$, with $f(0) = 0$, is continuous at $x = 0$.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. $f$ is a sum of continuous functions. At $x = 1$ the one-sided slopes are $-2$ and $0$; at $x = 2$ they are $0$ and $2$. So there are exactly two corners.\n(ii) Correct. $[x]$ is continuous at non-integers and jumps at integers; at 2, $g$ has left limit 1 and right limit 0.\n(iii) Wrong. The one-sided derivatives at 0 are $-1$ and $1$.\n(iv) Wrong. $|x|$ at 0 is a counterexample.\n(v) Wrong. It holds only for $x > 0$.\n(vi) Correct. $|x^2\\sin\\frac{1}{x}| \\le x^2 \\to 0 = f(0)$.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":62,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (function, on the stated interval) with List-II (derivative).\nList-I:\n(a) $\\cos^{-1}(\\sin x)$, $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} < x < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$\n(b) $\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\sin x}{1+\\cos x}\\right)$, $-\\pi < x < \\pi$\n(c) $\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2x}{1+x^2}\\right)$, $-1 < x < 1$\n(d) $\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3x-x^3}{1-3x^2}\\right)$, $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} < x < \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$\nList-II:\n(i) $\\frac{3}{1+x^2}$\n(ii) $-1$\n(iii) $\\frac{1}{2}$\n(iv) $\\frac{2}{1+x^2}$","image":"","options":["a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i","a-iii, b-ii, c-iv, d-i","a-ii, b-iii, c-i, d-iv","a-ii, b-iv, c-iii, d-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) $\\sin x = \\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{2} - x)$ with $\\frac{\\pi}{2} - x \\in (0, \\pi)$, so $f = \\frac{\\pi}{2} - x$ and $f' = -1$ → (ii).\n(b) $\\frac{\\sin x}{1+\\cos x} = \\tan\\frac{x}{2}$ with $\\frac{x}{2} \\in (-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2})$, so $f = \\frac{x}{2}$ and $f' = \\frac{1}{2}$ → (iii).\n(c) Put $x = \\tan\\theta$: $f = \\sin^{-1}(\\sin 2\\theta) = 2\\tan^{-1}x$, so $f' = \\frac{2}{1+x^2}$ → (iv).\n(d) Put $x = \\tan\\theta$: $f = \\tan^{-1}(\\tan 3\\theta) = 3\\tan^{-1}x$, so $f' = \\frac{3}{1+x^2}$ → (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":63,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $y=\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2x}{1+x^2}\\right)$, then the value of $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at $x=\\sqrt3$ is:","image":"","options":["$-\\frac{1}{2}$","$\\frac{1}{2}$","$-1$","$1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Put $x=\\tan\\theta$. Then $\\frac{2x}{1+x^2}=\\sin2\\theta$.\nFor $x>1$, $\\theta\\in\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$, so $2\\theta\\in\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\pi\\right)$, outside the principal range of $\\sin^{-1}$.\nTherefore $y=\\sin^{-1}(\\sin(\\pi-2\\theta))=\\pi-2\\theta=\\pi-2\\tan^{-1}x$.\nThis gives $\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{2}{1+x^2}$, and at $x=\\sqrt3$ it equals $-\\frac{2}{4}=-\\frac{1}{2}$.\nThe tempting answer $\\frac{1}{2}$ wrongly uses $y=2\\tan^{-1}x$, which is valid only for $|x|\\le1$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":64,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are continuous functions, that is, continuous at every point of their own domain?\n(i) $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}$, $x\\neq1$\n(ii) $f(x)=[x]$, $x\\in(0,1)$\n(iii) $f(x)=x[x]$, $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$\n(iv) $f(x)=x^2\\sin\\frac1x$ for $x\\neq0$, $f(0)=0$\n(v) $f(x)=x+1$ for $x<0$, $f(x)=x-1$ for $x>0$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Continuous: it is a rational function on its domain, and $x=1$ is excluded.\n(ii) Continuous: $[x]=0$ throughout $(0,1)$.\n(iii) Not continuous: at $x=1$, LHL $=1\\cdot0=0$ while RHL $=1\\cdot1=1$.\n(iv) Continuous: $|x^2\\sin\\frac1x|\\le x^2\\to0=f(0)$, and it is continuous elsewhere.\n(v) Continuous: $0$ is not in the domain, and each piece is continuous.\nSo 4 functions are continuous.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":65,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $y = (\\sin x)^{\\sin x}$ for $0<x<\\pi$, the value of $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at $x = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$ is approximately (take $\\log 2 = 0.693$, $\\sqrt2 = 1.414$, $\\sqrt3 = 1.732$):","image":"","options":["$0.188$","$0.613$","$-0.188$","$0.866$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$, $\\sin x=\\frac12$ and $\\cos x = \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$, so $y = (1/2)^{1/2} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\approx0.707$.\nFormula: $\\log y = \\sin x\\log\\sin x$ gives $\\frac{dy}{dx} = y\\cos x(1+\\log\\sin x)$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0.707\\times0.866\\times(1-0.693)$.\nSo $\\frac{dy}{dx} \\approx 0.188$.\nOption (2) omits the factor $\\cos x$; option (3) gets the sign of $1+\\log\\sin x$ wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":66,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x)=\\cos^{-1}(\\sin x)$ for $-\\frac{\\pi}{2}<x<\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. Then $f'(x)$ equals:","image":"","options":["$1$","$-1$","$0$","$-\\cos x$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Write $\\sin x=\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-x\\right)$.\nFor $-\\frac{\\pi}{2}<x<\\frac{\\pi}{2}$, the angle $\\frac{\\pi}{2}-x$ lies in $(0,\\pi)$, inside the principal range of $\\cos^{-1}$.\nSo $f(x)=\\cos^{-1}\\left(\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-x\\right)\\right)=\\frac{\\pi}{2}-x$.\nDifferentiating gives $f'(x)=-1$.\nCheck by the chain rule: $f'(x)=\\frac{-\\cos x}{\\sqrt{1-\\sin^2x}}=\\frac{-\\cos x}{|\\cos x|}=-1$, since $\\cos x>0$ here.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":67,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $y = x^x$, $x > 0$, find the value of $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}$ at $x = 1$.","image":"","options":["2","1","3","0"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Take logs: $\\log y = x\\log x$, so $\\frac{1}{y}y' = 1 + \\log x$. Hence $y' = y(1 + \\log x)$.\nDifferentiate again with the product rule: $y'' = y'(1 + \\log x) + y\\cdot\\frac{1}{x}$.\nAt $x = 1$: $y = 1$ and $\\log 1 = 0$, so $y' = 1$.\nSubstituting: $y'' = 1(1) + \\frac{1}{1} = 2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":68,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following functions are differentiable at $x = 0$?\n(i) $|x|^3$\n(ii) $x|x|$\n(iii) $|x|$\n(iv) $[x]$ (greatest integer function)\n(v) $\\sin|x|$\n(vi) $x^2$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Yes: $\\frac{|h|^3}{h} \\to 0$ from both sides.\n(ii) Yes: $\\frac{h|h|}{h} = |h| \\to 0$ from both sides.\n(iii) No: LHD $= -1$ and RHD $= 1$.\n(iv) No: $[x]$ is not even continuous at the integer 0, since the left limit is $-1$ and $f(0) = 0$.\n(v) No: $\\frac{\\sin|h|}{h} \\to -1$ from the left and $\\to 1$ from the right.\n(vi) Yes: the derivative is $2x$, which is 0 at $x=0$.\nThree functions qualify.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":69,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"How many points of discontinuity does the greatest integer function $f(x)=[x]$ have in the open interval $(-2, 3)$?","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Fact: $[x]$ is continuous at non-integers. It is discontinuous at every integer $c$, since LHL $=c-1$ and RHL $=c$.\nThe integers strictly between $-2$ and $3$ are $-1, 0, 1, 2$.\nThe endpoints $-2$ and $3$ are not included in the open interval.\nSo there are 4 points. Option (4) wrongly counts both endpoints.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":70,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $y = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2x}{1-x^2}\\right)$ for $-1 < x < 1$, then the value of $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at $x = \\frac{1}{2}$ is:","image":"","options":["$1.6$","$0.8$","$3.2$","$2.4$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Put $x = \\tan\\theta$ with $\\theta \\in (-\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{4})$.\nThen $\\frac{2\\tan\\theta}{1-\\tan^2\\theta} = \\tan 2\\theta$, and $2\\theta \\in (-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2})$.\nSo $y = 2\\theta = 2\\tan^{-1}x$, which gives $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2}{1+x^2}$.\nSubstituting $x = \\frac{1}{2}$: $\\frac{2}{1.25} = 1.6$.\nThe option 0.8 forgets the factor 2.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":71,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $y = \\log_7(\\log x)$ for x > 1, then the value of $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at $x = e$ is:","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{e\\log 7}$","$\\frac{\\log 7}{e}$","$\\frac{1}{e}$","$\\frac{1}{7e}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Change of base: $y = \\frac{\\log(\\log x)}{\\log 7}$.\nDifferentiate with the chain rule: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{1}{\\log 7}\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\log x}\\cdot\\frac{1}{x} = \\frac{1}{x\\log 7\\,\\log x}$.\nAt x = e, $\\log e = 1$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{1}{e\\log 7}$.\nOption (2) inverts the factor $\\log 7$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":72,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The function $f(x) = |x|$ is continuous at $x = 0$.\nReason (R): Every differentiable function is continuous.\n\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true: the left hand limit at 0 is $\\lim_{x\\to 0^-}(-x) = 0$, the right hand limit is $\\lim_{x\\to 0^+}x = 0$, and $f(0) = 0$.\nR is true: this is the corollary of Theorem 3 in NCERT.\nR does not explain A. The left hand derivative of $|x|$ at 0 is $-1$ and the right hand derivative is $1$, so $|x|$ is not differentiable at 0. Its continuity there must be checked directly from the limits.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":73,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The function $g(x)=x-[x]$ is discontinuous at every integer.\nReason (R): If c is an integer, then $\\lim_{x\\to c^-} g(x)=1$ whereas $g(c)=0$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Take c to be an integer.\nFor x slightly less than c, $[x]=c-1$, so $g(x)\\to c-(c-1)=1$.\nAt the point itself, $g(c)=c-c=0$.\nSince LHL = 1 ≠ 0 = g(c), g is discontinuous at every integer. So R is true.\nR is exactly the reason A holds, so it correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":74,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"A differentiable function $f$ satisfies $f'(t)=\\sqrt{1-t^2}$ for $-1<t<1$. If $F(x)=f(\\sin x)$, then $F'\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)$ equals","image":"","options":["$-\\frac{1}{4}$","$\\frac{1}{4}$","$-\\frac{1}{2}$","$\\frac{1}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Chain rule: $F'(x)=f'(\\sin x)\\cdot\\cos x=\\sqrt{1-\\sin^2 x}\\,\\cos x=|\\cos x|\\cos x$.\nAt $x=\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$: $\\cos x=-\\frac{1}{2}$, so $|\\cos x|=\\frac{1}{2}$.\nSo $F'\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{4}$.\nOption 2 comes from writing $\\sqrt{1-\\sin^2 x}=\\cos x$, which fails here because $\\cos x<0$ in the second quadrant.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":75,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $y = (\\sin^{-1}x)^2$ for $-1 < x < 1$, then the value of $(1 - x^2)\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} - x\\frac{dy}{dx}$ is:","image":"","options":["0","1","2","$-2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"First derivative: $y_1 = \\frac{2\\sin^{-1}x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$, so $\\sqrt{1-x^2}\\,y_1 = 2\\sin^{-1}x$.\nDifferentiate both sides: $\\sqrt{1-x^2}\\,y_2 - \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}y_1 = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$.\nMultiply through by $\\sqrt{1-x^2}$: $(1-x^2)y_2 - xy_1 = 2$.\nThe value 0 holds for $y = \\sin^{-1}x$, not for its square.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":76,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"For $f(x) = |x-1|$, the left hand derivative and the right hand derivative at $x = 1$ are, respectively:","image":"","options":["$-1$ and $1$","$1$ and $-1$","$0$ and $0$","$1$ and $1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Near $x=1$: $f(x) = 1-x$ for $x<1$ and $f(x) = x-1$ for $x \\ge 1$, with $f(1)=0$.\nLHD $= \\lim_{h \\to 0^-} \\frac{|h| - 0}{h} = \\lim_{h \\to 0^-} \\frac{-h}{h} = -1$.\nRHD $= \\lim_{h \\to 0^+} \\frac{h}{h} = 1$.\nThe two values are finite but unequal, so f is not differentiable at x = 1. The function is still continuous there.\nOption (2) reverses the order of the two derivatives.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":77,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"For the function $f(x) = |x - 2|$, what is the left hand derivative at $x = 2$?","image":"","options":["$-1$","$1$","$0$","$2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $f(x) = |x-2|$. For $x < 2$, $f(x) = 2 - x$.\nFormula: LHD $= \\lim_{h \\to 0^-} \\frac{f(2+h) - f(2)}{h}$.\nSubstituting: for $h < 0$, $f(2+h) = |h| = -h$ and $f(2) = 0$, so the quotient is $\\frac{-h}{h} = -1$.\nSo LHD $= -1$. The RHD is $+1$, which is the tempting wrong option.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":78,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $y = e^{2x} + x^3$, then the value of $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}$ at $x = 0$ is","image":"","options":["2","4","6","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $y = e^{2x} + x^3$.\nFirst derivative (chain rule): $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 2e^{2x} + 3x^2$.\nSecond derivative: $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = 4e^{2x} + 6x$.\nSubstituting $x = 0$: $4e^0 + 6(0) = 4$.\nThe tempting value 2 comes from forgetting to apply the chain rule a second time.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":79,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x) = x^2$ for $x \\le 1$ and $f(x) = ax + b$ for $x > 1$.\nStatement I: $f$ is differentiable at $x = 1$ if and only if $a = 2$ and $b = -1$.\nStatement II: The function $g(x) = |x| + |x - 1|$ is continuous everywhere but not differentiable at exactly two points.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Differentiability needs continuity first. $f(1)=1$ and the right hand limit is $a+b$, so $a+b=1$.\nThe left hand derivative is $2x$ at 1, which is 2. The right hand derivative is $a$. Equal derivatives give $a=2$, and then $b=-1$. Statement I is true.\nStatement II: $g$ is a sum of continuous functions, so it is continuous. Its slope is $-2$ for $x<0$, $0$ for $0<x<1$, and $2$ for $x>1$.\nThe slopes jump at $x=0$ and $x=1$ only, so $g$ fails to be differentiable at exactly two points. Statement II is true.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":80,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"For the parabola given parametrically by $x = at^2$, $y = 2at$ ($a > 0$, $t \\neq 0$), $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}$ equals:","image":"","options":["$-\\frac{1}{t^2}$","$-\\frac{1}{2at^3}$","$\\frac{1}{2at^3}$","$-\\frac{1}{2at^2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"First derivative: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{dy/dt}{dx/dt} = \\frac{2a}{2at} = \\frac{1}{t}$.\nSecond derivative: $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = \\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right) \\cdot \\frac{dt}{dx} = -\\frac{1}{t^2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2at} = -\\frac{1}{2at^3}$.\nOption (1) is a common mistake: it differentiates $\\frac{1}{t}$ with respect to t but forgets to multiply by $\\frac{dt}{dx}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":81,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"For the curve $x = a(\\theta + \\sin\\theta)$, $y = a(1 - \\cos\\theta)$, the value of $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at $\\theta = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}$ is:","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$","$-\\sqrt{3}$","$1$","$\\sqrt{3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta} = a(1 + \\cos\\theta)$ and $\\frac{dy}{d\\theta} = a\\sin\\theta$.\nFormula: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{a\\sin\\theta}{a(1 + \\cos\\theta)} = \\frac{2\\sin\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\cos\\frac{\\theta}{2}}{2\\cos^2\\frac{\\theta}{2}} = \\tan\\frac{\\theta}{2}$.\nSubstituting $\\theta = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}$: $\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\sqrt{3}$.\nCheck directly: $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}/2}{1 - 1/2} = \\sqrt{3}$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":82,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"A curve is given parametrically by $x = 2t^3$, $y = 3t^2$. Find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at $t = 2$.","image":"","options":["0.5","2","1","0.25"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $x = 2t^3$, $y = 3t^2$.\nFormula: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{dy/dt}{dx/dt}$, provided $\\frac{dx}{dt} \\ne 0$.\nHere $\\frac{dx}{dt} = 6t^2$ and $\\frac{dy}{dt} = 6t$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{6t}{6t^2} = \\frac{1}{t}$.\nSubstituting $t = 2$: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0.5$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":83,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $y = x \\log x$ for $x > 0$, then $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}$ equals:","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{x}$","$\\log x$","$-\\frac{1}{x^2}$","$1 + \\log x$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"By the product rule: $y' = x \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} + \\log x \\cdot 1 = 1 + \\log x$.\nDifferentiating again: $y'' = 0 + \\frac{1}{x} = \\frac{1}{x}$.\nOption (4) is only the first derivative.\nOption (3) is the second derivative of $\\log x$ itself, not of $x\\log x$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":84,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"A curve is given parametrically by $x = 2\\sec\\theta$, $y = 3\\tan\\theta$. What is the value of $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$?","image":"","options":["$3$","$\\frac{1}{3}$","$\\sqrt{3}$","$\\frac{3}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $x = 2\\sec\\theta$ and $y = 3\\tan\\theta$.\nFormula: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{dy/d\\theta}{dx/d\\theta}$, valid when $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta} \\neq 0$.\nDifferentiating: $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta} = 2\\sec\\theta\\tan\\theta$ and $\\frac{dy}{d\\theta} = 3\\sec^2\\theta$.\nSo $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{3\\sec^2\\theta}{2\\sec\\theta\\tan\\theta} = \\frac{3\\sec\\theta}{2\\tan\\theta} = \\frac{3}{2\\sin\\theta}$.\nSubstituting $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$, where $\\sin\\theta = \\frac{1}{2}$: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{3}{2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}} = 3$.\nThe option $\\frac{1}{3}$ comes from dividing the derivatives the wrong way round, which gives $\\frac{dx}{dy}$ instead.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":85,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $f(x)=\\begin{cases} ax+2, & x\\le 2 \\\\ bx-4, & x>2 \\end{cases}$ is continuous at $x=2$, then $a$ and $b$ are related by","image":"","options":["$a-b=3$","$a+b=3$","$b-a=3$","$a=b$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Value and left hand limit at 2: $f(2)=2a+2$.\nRight hand limit: $\\lim_{x\\to 2^+}(bx-4)=2b-4$.\nContinuity requires $2a+2=2b-4$, so $2b-2a=6$.\nThis gives $b-a=3$. The option $a-b=3$ has the sign reversed.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":86,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Consider $g(x) = x|x|$ for all real $x$. Which of the following statements are correct?\n(i) $g$ is continuous at $x = 0$.\n(ii) $g$ is differentiable at $x = 0$ and $g'(0) = 0$.\n(iii) $g'$ is differentiable at $x = 0$.","image":"","options":["(i) and (ii) only","(i) only","(i), (ii) and (iii)","(ii) and (iii) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Write $g(x) = -x^2$ for $x < 0$ and $g(x) = x^2$ for $x \\ge 0$.\n(i) Correct: both one-sided limits are 0, and $g(0) = 0$.\n(ii) Correct: the difference quotient is $\\frac{h|h|}{h} = |h|$, which tends to 0 from both sides. So $g'(0) = 0$.\n(iii) Incorrect: $g'(x) = 2|x|$, whose left and right derivatives at 0 are $-2$ and $2$. These differ, so $g'$ is not differentiable at 0.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":87,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Consider $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$, $x\\ne 0$. Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["$\\lim_{x\\to 0^+} f(x)=+\\infty$ is a real number, so the right hand limit of f at 0 exists","f is not a continuous function, because its graph cannot be drawn without lifting the pen at x = 0","f is continuous at every point of its domain, and its right hand limit at 0 does not exist as a real number","The left hand limit of f at 0 is $+\\infty$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Wrong: $+\\infty$ is NOT a real number, so this limit does not exist as a real number.\n(2) Wrong: 0 is not in the domain of f. Continuity is checked only at points of the domain, so lifting the pen at 0 does not matter.\n(3) Correct: for any $c\\ne0$, $\\lim_{x\\to c}\\frac1x=\\frac1c=f(c)$, so f is continuous on its domain. Also $\\lim_{x\\to0^+}f(x)=+\\infty$, which is not real.\n(4) Wrong: as $x\\to0^-$, $f(x)\\to-\\infty$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":88,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"For a base $b > 1$, which of the following statements about the logarithmic function $y = \\log_b x$ is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Its domain is the set of all positive real numbers","Its range is the set of all real numbers","The point $(1, 0)$ lies on its graph for every base $b > 1$","As $x \\to 0^+$, its graph approaches the x-axis in the fourth quadrant"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the logarithm of a non-positive number is not defined, so the domain is $R^+$.\n(2) Correct: the range is R.\n(3) Correct: $b^0=1$ gives $\\log_b 1=0$.\n(4) Incorrect: as $x\\to0^+$, $\\log_b x$ becomes smaller than any given real number. So the graph approaches the y-axis in the fourth quadrant, never meeting it.\nIt is the exponential function whose graph approaches the x-axis, in the second quadrant.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":89,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the logarithmic function $y = \\log_b x$ with $b > 1$. How many of the following statements are true?\n(i) Its domain is $R^+$.\n(ii) Its range is $R^+$.\n(iii) Its graph passes through the point (1, 0).\n(iv) It is ever increasing.\n(v) Its graph meets the y-axis at some point.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) True: the logarithm of a non-positive number is not defined.\n(ii) False: the range is all real numbers.\n(iii) True: $b^0 = 1$, so $\\log_b 1 = 0$.\n(iv) True: the graph rises from left to right.\n(v) False: as x tends to 0 from the right, the graph approaches the y-axis in the fourth quadrant but never meets it.\nSo 3 statements are true.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":90,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Let $x = a^{\\sin^{-1}t}$ and $y = a^{\\cos^{-1}t}$, where $a > 1$ and $-1 < t < 1$.\nStatement I: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{y}{x}$.\nStatement II: The product $xy$ has the same value for every $t$ in $(-1, 1)$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement II is true: $xy = a^{\\sin^{-1}t + \\cos^{-1}t} = a^{\\pi/2}$, which does not depend on $t$.\nStatement I is true. By the chain rule, $\\frac{dx}{dt} = \\frac{a^{\\sin^{-1}t}\\log a}{\\sqrt{1 - t^2}}$ and $\\frac{dy}{dt} = -\\frac{a^{\\cos^{-1}t}\\log a}{\\sqrt{1 - t^2}}$.\nDividing, $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{a^{\\cos^{-1}t}}{a^{\\sin^{-1}t}} = -\\frac{y}{x}$.\nThis agrees with differentiating $xy = $ constant.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":91,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (function) with List-II (derivative with respect to x), in appropriate domains.\nList-I:\n(a) $\\sin(\\log x)$\n(b) $\\cos^{-1}(e^x)$\n(c) $\\log(\\log x)$\n(d) $e^{\\cos x}$\nList-II:\n(i) $\\frac{-e^x}{\\sqrt{1-e^{2x}}}$\n(ii) $-\\sin x\\, e^{\\cos x}$\n(iii) $\\frac{\\cos(\\log x)}{x}$\n(iv) $\\frac{1}{x\\log x}$","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) $\\cos(\\log x)\\cdot\\frac{1}{x}$, which is (iii).\n(b) $\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{1-(e^x)^2}}\\cdot e^x$, which is (i).\n(c) $\\frac{1}{\\log x}\\cdot\\frac{1}{x}$, which is (iv).\n(d) $e^{\\cos x}\\cdot(-\\sin x)$, which is (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":92,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"The curve $xy + y^2 = \\tan x + 4$ passes through the point (0, 2). The slope $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ of the curve at this point is","image":"","options":["$0.25$","$-0.5$","$-0.25$","$0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $xy + y^2 = \\tan x + 4$ at (0, 2). Check: $0 + 4 = 0 + 4$, so the point is on the curve.\nFormula: differentiate implicitly, $y + x\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\sec^2 x$.\nSubstituting x = 0, y = 2: $2 + 0 + 4\\frac{dy}{dx} = 1$, so $4\\frac{dy}{dx} = -1$.\nSo $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -0.25$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":93,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (function) with List-II (derivative).\nList-I:\n(a) $\\sin^{-1}x$\n(b) $\\tan^{-1}x$\n(c) $e^{-x}$\n(d) $\\log x$\nList-II:\n(i) $\\frac{1}{1+x^2}$\n(ii) $\\frac{1}{x}$\n(iii) $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$\n(iv) $-e^{-x}$","image":"","options":["a-i, b-iii, c-iv, d-ii","a-iii, b-i, c-ii, d-iv","a-iv, b-i, c-iii, d-ii","a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Match each pair:\n(a) $\\frac{d}{dx}\\sin^{-1}x = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$ for $x \\in (-1, 1)$, so a-iii.\n(b) $\\frac{d}{dx}\\tan^{-1}x = \\frac{1}{1+x^2}$, so b-i.\n(c) By the chain rule, $\\frac{d}{dx}e^{-x} = e^{-x}\\cdot(-1) = -e^{-x}$, so c-iv.\n(d) $\\frac{d}{dx}\\log x = \\frac{1}{x}$, so d-ii.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":94,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The function $f(x) = |x|$ is continuous at $x = 0$ but is not differentiable at $x = 0$.\nReason (R): If a function is continuous at a point $c$, then it must be differentiable at $c$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. At 0, the left hand limit, the right hand limit and $f(0)$ all equal 0, so $|x|$ is continuous there. However, LHD $= -1$ and RHD $= 1$, so it is not differentiable at 0.\nR is false. Theorem 3 runs only one way: differentiable implies continuous. Its converse does not hold, and $|x|$ itself is a counterexample.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":95,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For $x>0$, $y = x^{\\sin x}$ can be differentiated by first writing $\\log y = \\sin x \\log x$.\nReason (R): Logarithmic differentiation of $f(x)=[u(x)]^{v(x)}$ makes sense only when both $f(x)$ and $u(x)$ are positive, since the logarithm of a non-positive number is not defined.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: For $x>0$, the base $x$ is positive, so $y = x^{\\sin x}>0$. Taking logs is valid and gives $\\log y = \\sin x\\log x$. A is true.\nR: NCERT stresses that f(x) and u(x) must be positive, otherwise their logarithms are undefined. R is true.\nR is exactly the reason A is valid, because the condition holds when $x>0$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":96,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following functions are differentiable at $x = 0$?\n(i) $|x|$\n(ii) $x|x|$\n(iii) $|x|^3$\n(iv) $\\sin|x|$\n(v) $|\\sin x|$","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Not differentiable: the left hand derivative is $-1$ and the right hand derivative is $1$.\n(ii) Differentiable: $\\frac{f(h)-f(0)}{h}=\\frac{h|h|}{h}=|h|\\to0$ from both sides.\n(iii) Differentiable: $\\frac{|h|^3}{h}\\to0$ from both sides.\n(iv) Not differentiable: $\\frac{\\sin|h|}{h}\\to -1$ as $h\\to0^-$ and $\\to 1$ as $h\\to0^+$.\n(v) Not differentiable: $\\frac{|\\sin h|}{h}\\to -1$ as $h\\to0^-$ and $\\to 1$ as $h\\to0^+$.\nSo exactly 2 functions are differentiable at 0.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":97,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $y = (\\log x)^x$ for $x>1$, then $\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{dy}{dx}$ equals:","image":"","options":["$\\frac{x}{\\log x}$","$\\log(\\log x) + \\frac{1}{\\log x}$","$\\log(\\log x) + \\frac{1}{x\\log x}$","$x(\\log x)^{x-1}\\cdot\\frac1x$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Take logs: $\\log y = x\\log(\\log x)$. This is valid because $\\log x>0$ for $x>1$.\nDifferentiate using the product rule: $\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\log(\\log x) + x\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\log x}\\cdot\\frac1x$.\nThis simplifies to $\\log(\\log x)+\\frac{1}{\\log x}$.\nOption (3) forgets that the factor x multiplies the $\\frac{1}{x\\log x}$ term, so it does not cancel the $\\frac1x$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":98,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"The function $f(x)=\\begin{cases} kx+1, & x\\le 2\\pi \\\\ \\cos x, & x>2\\pi \\end{cases}$ is continuous at $x=2\\pi$. The value of $k$ is","image":"","options":["$0$","$-\\frac{2}{\\pi}$","$\\frac{1}{2\\pi}$","$-\\frac{1}{\\pi}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Left hand limit at $2\\pi$ equals the value of the function: $f(2\\pi)=2\\pi k+1$.\nRight hand limit: $\\lim_{x\\to 2\\pi^+}\\cos x=\\cos 2\\pi=1$.\nFor continuity, set them equal: $2\\pi k+1=1$, so $k=0$.\nThe option $-\\frac{2}{\\pi}$ is the trap. It comes from matching at $x=\\pi$, where $\\cos\\pi=-1$, not at $2\\pi$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":99,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x) = \\log(e^x)$ and $g(x) = e^{\\log x}$, where log denotes the natural logarithm. Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["f and g are identical functions","$f'(x) = 1$ for all real x, and $g'(x) = 1$ only for x > 0, because g is defined only for x > 0","$g'(x) = 1$ for every real x","f is defined only for x > 0"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"f: $e^x > 0$ for all real x, so $\\log(e^x) = x\\log e = x$ for every real x. The domain is R and $f'(x) = 1$.\ng: $\\log x$ needs x > 0, so the domain is $(0,\\infty)$. There $g(x) = x$ and $g'(x) = 1$.\nThe rules are the same but the domains differ, so the functions are not identical. This rules out (1).\n(3) fails because g does not exist for x ≤ 0. (4) fails because f is defined for all real x.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":100,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"The greatest integer function $f(x) = [x]$, defined for $0 < x < 3$, is NOT differentiable at:","image":"","options":["$x = 1.5$ only","$x = 1$ and $x = 2$ only","every point of $(0, 3)$","no point of $(0, 3)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"At any non-integer $c$, $[x]$ is constant near $c$, so it is differentiable there with derivative 0. This rules out options (1) and (3).\nAt an integer $c$, the left hand limit of $[x]$ is $c - 1$ and the right hand limit is $c$. So $[x]$ is discontinuous at $c$.\nBy Theorem 3, a function that is not continuous at a point cannot be differentiable there.\nThe only integers in $(0, 3)$ are 1 and 2.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":101,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"The function $f(x)=\\begin{cases}2x+k, & x\\le 1\\\\ x^2+3, & x>1\\end{cases}$ is continuous at $x=1$. The value of $k$ is:","image":"","options":["1","2","4","-2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: f is continuous at x = 1.\nCondition: LHL = RHL = f(1).\nLHL = f(1) = $2(1)+k = 2+k$.\nRHL = $\\lim_{x\\to 1^+}(x^2+3) = 1+3 = 4$.\nSo $2+k = 4$, which gives k = 2.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":102,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (functions, with domains) with List-II (derivatives):\nList-I\n(A) $\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\sin x}{1+\\cos x}\\right)$, $-\\pi<x<\\pi$\n(B) $\\sec^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2x^2-1}\\right)$, $0<x<\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}$\n(C) $\\sin^{-1}\\left(2x\\sqrt{1-x^2}\\right)$, $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}<x<\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}$\n(D) $\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2x}{1-x^2}\\right)$, $-1<x<1$\nList-II\n(I) $\\frac{2}{1+x^2}$\n(II) $\\frac{1}{2}$\n(III) $-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$\n(IV) $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(A) $\\frac{\\sin x}{1+\\cos x}=\\tan\\frac{x}{2}$ with $\\frac{x}{2}\\in\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$, so the function is $\\frac{x}{2}$ and its derivative is $\\frac{1}{2}$: II.\n(B) Put $x=\\cos\\theta$ with $\\theta\\in\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$. The argument is $\\sec2\\theta$ with $2\\theta\\in\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\pi\\right)$, so the function is $2\\cos^{-1}x$, derivative $-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$: III.\n(C) Put $x=\\sin\\theta$ with $2\\theta\\in\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$. The function is $2\\sin^{-1}x$, derivative $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$: IV.\n(D) Put $x=\\tan\\theta$ with $2\\theta\\in\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$. The function is $2\\tan^{-1}x$, derivative $\\frac{2}{1+x^2}$: I.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":103,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"For the exponential function $y = b^x$ with base $b > 1$, which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Its domain is the set of all real numbers","Its range is the set of all positive real numbers","Its graph always passes through the point (0, 1)","For very large negative values of x, its graph meets the x-axis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: $b^x$ is defined for every real x, so the domain is R.\n(2) Correct: $b^x > 0$ always, so the range is the positive reals.\n(3) Correct: $b^0 = 1$, so (0, 1) lies on the graph.\n(4) Incorrect: in the second quadrant the graph comes very close to the x-axis but never meets it, because $b^x$ is never 0.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":104,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"For $x>0$, the derivative of $y = x^x$ with respect to $x$ is:","image":"","options":["$x \\cdot x^{x-1}$","$x^x \\log x$","$x^x(1+\\log x)$","$\\frac{x^x}{1+\\log x}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Take logs: $\\log y = x\\log x$.\nDifferentiate both sides: $\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{dy}{dx} = x\\cdot\\frac{1}{x} + \\log x = 1+\\log x$.\nSo $\\frac{dy}{dx} = x^x(1+\\log x)$.\nOption (1) wrongly uses the power rule, and option (2) wrongly uses the $a^x$ rule. Neither rule applies on its own, because both the base and the exponent vary with x.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":105,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $y = 2^{\\,t + \\frac{1}{t}}$ and $x = \\left(t + \\frac{1}{t}\\right)^2$, then the value of $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at $t = 2$ is closest to (take $\\log_e 2 = 0.693$):","image":"","options":["$1.568$","$1.131$","$0.784$","$0.392$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: let $s = t + \\frac{1}{t}$, so $\\frac{ds}{dt} = 1 - \\frac{1}{t^2}$. At $t = 2$, $s = 2.5$.\nFormula: $\\frac{dy}{dt} = 2^s\\log 2\\cdot\\frac{ds}{dt}$ and $\\frac{dx}{dt} = 2s\\cdot\\frac{ds}{dt}$.\nThe factor $\\frac{ds}{dt} = \\frac{3}{4} \\neq 0$ cancels, so $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2^s\\log 2}{2s}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{2^{2.5} \\times 0.693}{5} = \\frac{5.657 \\times 0.693}{5} \\approx 0.784$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":106,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For the relation $y + \\sin y = \\cos x$, the expression for $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ obtained by implicit differentiation is not defined when $y = (2n+1)\\pi$, $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\nReason (R): $1 + \\cos y = 0$ when $y = (2n+1)\\pi$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Differentiating implicitly: $\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\cos y \\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\sin x$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{\\sin x}{1 + \\cos y}$.\nThis expression fails exactly when the denominator $1 + \\cos y$ is zero.\nR is true: $\\cos((2n+1)\\pi) = -1$, so $1 + \\cos y = 0$.\nA is true, and the reason it is true is precisely the zero denominator stated in R. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":107,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): If $x = at^2$ and $y = 2at$, then $\\frac{dy}{dx} = t$.\nReason (R): For a curve $x = f(t)$, $y = g(t)$, we have $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{g'(t)}{f'(t)}$, provided $f'(t) \\neq 0$.\n\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"R is true: by the chain rule, $\\frac{dy}{dt} = \\frac{dy}{dx}\\cdot\\frac{dx}{dt}$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{g'(t)}{f'(t)}$ whenever $f'(t) \\neq 0$.\nNow apply R to the given curve. Here $\\frac{dx}{dt} = 2at$ and $\\frac{dy}{dt} = 2a$.\nSo $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2a}{2at} = \\frac{1}{t}$, not $t$. Hence A is false.\nThe tempting slip is writing $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ over $\\frac{dy}{dt}$, which inverts the ratio.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":108,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following results are correct (with $\\log$ denoting the natural logarithm and $a > 0$ a constant)?\n(i) $\\frac{d}{dx}(a^x) = a^x \\log a$\n(ii) $\\frac{d}{dx}(x^x) = x \\cdot x^{x-1}$ for $x > 0$\n(iii) $\\frac{d}{dx}(e^{-x}) = -e^{-x}$\n(iv) $\\frac{d}{dx}\\log(\\log x) = \\frac{1}{x \\log x}$ for $x > 1$\n(v) $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\log_7 x) = \\frac{1}{x \\log 7}$ for $x > 0$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: from $\\log y=x\\log a$ we get $y'=y\\log a$.\n(ii) Incorrect: from $\\log y=x\\log x$ we get $\\frac{y'}{y}=1+\\log x$, so $y'=x^x(1+\\log x)$. The power rule does not apply because the exponent is variable.\n(iii) Correct: by the chain rule, $e^{-x}\\cdot(-1)$.\n(iv) Correct: $\\frac{1}{\\log x}\\cdot\\frac{1}{x}$.\n(v) Correct: $\\log_7 x=\\frac{\\log x}{\\log 7}$.\nSo 4 results are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":109,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the exponential function $y = b^x$ $(b > 1)$ and the logarithmic function $y = \\log_b x$ is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The domain of $\\log_b x$ is the set of positive real numbers","The graph of $y = b^x$ always passes through the point $(0, 1)$","The range of $\\log_b x$ is the set of positive real numbers","The graphs of $y = e^x$ and $y = \\log x$ are mirror images of each other in the line $y = x$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the log of a non-positive number is not defined, so the domain is $R^+$.\n(2) Correct: $b^0 = 1$, so $(0, 1)$ lies on every exponential graph.\n(3) Incorrect: the range of the log function is all real numbers. For $0 < x < 1$ its values are negative. It is the range of $b^x$ that is the positive reals, which is why this option is tempting.\n(4) Correct: $e^x$ and $\\log x$ are inverse functions, so their graphs reflect in $y = x$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":110,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following functions are continuous at $x=2$?\n(i) $[x]$\n(ii) $x-[x]$\n(iii) $|x-2|$\n(iv) $\\frac{x^2-4}{x-2}$ for $x\\ne2$, and 4 at $x=2$\n(v) $\\frac{1}{x-2}$ for $x\\ne2$, and 0 at $x=2$\n(vi) $\\sin(x^2)$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Not continuous: 2 is an integer, and LHL = 1 ≠ RHL = 2.\n(ii) Not continuous: LHL = 1 but the value at 2 is 0.\n(iii) Continuous: the modulus of a polynomial is continuous everywhere.\n(iv) Continuous: for x ≠ 2 the function equals x + 2, whose limit is 4, matching f(2) = 4.\n(v) Not continuous: the one-sided limits are ±∞, which are not real numbers.\n(vi) Continuous: it is a composite of continuous functions.\nThat makes 3 continuous functions.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":111,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $f(x)=\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi x^2}{4}\\right)$, then $f'(1)$ equals","image":"","options":["$\\frac{\\pi\\sqrt{2}}{4}$","$\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$","$\\frac{\\pi\\sqrt{2}}{8}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $f(x)=\\sin t$ with $t=\\frac{\\pi x^2}{4}$, so $\\frac{dt}{dx}=\\frac{\\pi x}{2}$.\nFormula: $f'(x)=\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi x^2}{4}\\right)\\cdot\\frac{\\pi x}{2}$.\nSubstituting $x=1$: $f'(1)=\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\cdot\\frac{\\pi}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\nSo $f'(1)=\\frac{\\pi\\sqrt{2}}{4}$. Option 2 omits the inner derivative.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":112,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"The number of points in the open interval $(-2, 2)$ at which $f(x)=x[x]$ is discontinuous, where $[x]$ is the greatest integer function, is:","image":"","options":["3","1","4","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"f can fail to be continuous only at the integers −1, 0 and 1. Elsewhere [x] is locally constant, so f is continuous.\nAt x = −1: LHL = (−1)(−2) = 2 and RHL = (−1)(−1) = 1. These differ, so f is discontinuous.\nAt x = 0: LHL = 0·(−1) = 0, RHL = 0·0 = 0 and f(0) = 0. So f is continuous here, which is the trap.\nAt x = 1: LHL = 1·0 = 0 and RHL = 1·1 = 1. These differ, so f is discontinuous.\nTotal: 2 points.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":113,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: To differentiate $y = (\\log x)^{\\cos x}$ by logarithmic differentiation (taking real logarithms of both sides), we must restrict to $x > 1$.\nStatement II: Logarithmic differentiation of $f(x) = [u(x)]^{v(x)}$ makes sense only when both $f(x)$ and $u(x)$ are positive.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement II is true. NCERT stresses that in $\\log y = v(x)\\log u(x)$, both $f(x)$ and $u(x)$ must be positive, otherwise the logarithms are undefined.\nStatement I is true. Here $u(x) = \\log x$, and $\\log x > 0$ only when $x > 1$. For $0 < x \\le 1$, $\\log(\\log x)$ is not defined.\nSo both statements hold, and Statement I is just Statement II applied to this function.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":114,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Consider $f(x)=\\begin{cases} \\lambda(x^2-3x), & x\\le 0 \\\\ 2x+5, & x>0 \\end{cases}$. Which of the following is correct about continuity of $f$ at $x=0$?","image":"","options":["$f$ is continuous at $x=0$ only when $\\lambda=5$","$f$ is continuous at $x=0$ only when $\\lambda=-5$","$f$ is continuous at $x=0$ for every real $\\lambda$","There is no real $\\lambda$ for which $f$ is continuous at $x=0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"$f(0)=\\lambda(0-0)=0$ for every $\\lambda$, so the left-hand limit is also 0.\nThe right-hand limit is $\\lim_{x\\to 0^+}(2x+5)=5$.\nLHL $=0\\ne 5=$ RHL, whatever the value of $\\lambda$.\nThe factor $\\lambda$ multiplies zero, so it cannot fix the jump. Hence no $\\lambda$ works.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":115,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following functions are continuous at every real number?\n(i) $\\sin x + \\cos x$\n(ii) $\\sin x \\cdot \\cos x$\n(iii) $|\\cos x|$\n(iv) $\\tan x$\n(v) $\\sin |x|$\n(vi) $\\text{cosec } x$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Continuous everywhere: it is the sum of two continuous functions.\n(ii) Continuous everywhere: it is the product of two continuous functions.\n(iii) Continuous everywhere: $|\\cdot|$ composed with cos x.\n(iv) Not defined at $x = (2n+1)\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. It is continuous on its domain, but not at every real number.\n(v) Continuous everywhere: sin composed with $|x|$.\n(vi) Not defined at $x = n\\pi$, so it is not continuous at every real number.\nCount = 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":116,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"A function is defined by $f(x) = ax^2 + bx$ for $x \\le 1$ and $f(x) = \\frac{3}{x}$ for $x > 1$. If $f$ is differentiable at $x = 1$, then the value of $ab$ is","image":"","options":["$-54$","$54$","$-18$","$18$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $f$ is differentiable at 1, so it must also be continuous at 1 (Theorem 3).\nContinuity: $f(1) = a + b$ and the RHL is $\\frac{3}{1} = 3$, so $a + b = 3$.\nEqual one-sided derivatives: the left derivative is $2a + b$. The right derivative is $-\\frac{3}{x^2} = -3$ at $x = 1$. So $2a + b = -3$.\nSolving: subtracting the equations gives $a = -6$, and then $b = 9$.\nSo $ab = -54$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":117,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about derivatives of functions in parametric form is correct?","image":"","options":["The formula $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{dy/dt}{dx/dt}$ can be used whenever $\\frac{dy}{dt} \\neq 0$","For $x = a\\cos\\theta$, $y = a\\sin\\theta$, the formula $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{dy/d\\theta}{dx/d\\theta}$ cannot be applied at $\\theta = 0$","A derivative found in parametric form can never be rewritten in terms of $x$ and $y$","For $x = at^2$, $y = 2at$, we get $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 2at$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: the condition needed is $\\frac{dx}{dt} \\neq 0$, not $\\frac{dy}{dt} \\neq 0$.\n(2) Correct: $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta} = -a\\sin\\theta = 0$ at $\\theta = 0$, so the quotient is not defined there.\n(3) Incorrect: for the astroid, $-\\tan\\theta$ can be rewritten as $-\\sqrt[3]{y/x}$.\n(4) Incorrect: $2at$ is $\\frac{dx}{dt}$. The actual slope is $\\frac{2a}{2at} = \\frac{1}{t}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":118,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Match each function in List-I (continuous at the indicated point) with the required value of $k$ in List-II.\nList-I\n(P) $f(x)=kx+3$ for $x\\le 4$, $2x-1$ for $x>4$; at $x=4$\n(Q) $f(x)=kx^2$ for $x\\le 2$, $3$ for $x>2$; at $x=2$\n(R) $f(x)=\\frac{x^2-4}{x-2}$ for $x\\ne 2$, $k$ for $x=2$; at $x=2$\n(S) $f(x)=kx-1$ for $x\\le 2$, $x^2$ for $x>2$; at $x=2$\nList-II\n(1) $1$\n(2) $\\frac{3}{4}$\n(3) $4$\n(4) $\\frac{5}{2}$","image":"","options":["P-1, Q-2, R-3, S-4","P-1, Q-4, R-3, S-2","P-3, Q-2, R-1, S-4","P-2, Q-1, R-3, S-4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(P) $4k+3=2(4)-1=7$, so $k=1$ → (1).\n(Q) $4k=3$, so $k=\\frac{3}{4}$ → (2).\n(R) For $x\\ne 2$, $\\frac{x^2-4}{x-2}=x+2$, which tends to 4. So $k=4$ → (3).\n(S) $2k-1=4$, so $k=\\frac{5}{2}$ → (4).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":119,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $f(x) = \\log_x 10$ for x > 0, x ≠ 1, then $f'(e)$ equals:","image":"","options":["$-\\frac{\\log 10}{e}$","$\\frac{\\log 10}{e}$","$-\\frac{1}{e\\log 10}$","$-\\frac{1}{e}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Change of base: $f(x) = \\frac{\\log 10}{\\log x} = \\log 10\\,(\\log x)^{-1}$.\nChain rule: $f'(x) = \\log 10\\cdot(-1)(\\log x)^{-2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{x} = -\\frac{\\log 10}{x(\\log x)^2}$.\nAt x = e: $\\log e = 1$, so $f'(e) = -\\frac{\\log 10}{e} \\approx -0.847$.\nOption (3) comes from wrongly writing $\\log_x 10$ as $\\frac{\\log x}{\\log 10}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":120,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following functions are continuous functions, i.e., continuous at every point of their own domain?\n(i) $f(x) = |x|$\n(ii) $f(x) = \\frac{1}{x}$, $x \\neq 0$\n(iii) $f(x) = \\tan x$\n(iv) $f(x) = [x]$, the greatest integer function\n(v) $f(x) = x + 2$ for $x < 0$ and $f(x) = -x + 2$ for $x > 0$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Continuous: $|x|$ is continuous at every real number.\n(ii) Continuous: $\\frac{1}{x}$ equals its limit at every $c \\neq 0$. The point 0 is not in its domain.\n(iii) Continuous: $\\tan x$ is a quotient of continuous functions, and it is continuous wherever it is defined.\n(iv) Not continuous: $[x]$ is defined at every integer $c$, but there the LHL is $c - 1$ and the RHL is $c$.\n(v) Continuous: 0 is not in the domain, and each piece is continuous on its part. You lift the pen only at a point where the function is undefined.\nCount = 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":121,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Consider $f(x) = [x]$, $0 < x < 3$, where $[x]$ is the greatest integer function.\nStatement I: At $x = 1$, the right hand derivative $\\lim_{h \\to 0^+} \\frac{f(1+h) - f(1)}{h}$ equals 0.\nStatement II: At $x = 1$, the left hand derivative $\\lim_{h \\to 0^-} \\frac{f(1+h) - f(1)}{h}$ equals $-1$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. For small $h>0$, $[1+h] = 1 = f(1)$, so the quotient is $\\frac{0}{h} = 0$ and the limit is 0.\nStatement II is incorrect. For small $h<0$, $[1+h] = 0$, so the quotient is $\\frac{0 - 1}{h} = -\\frac{1}{h}$. As $h \\to 0^-$, this tends to $+\\infty$, so the LHD is not finite.\nSince the LHD is not finite, $[x]$ is not differentiable at $x=1$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":122,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $f(x)=\\frac{\\sin 5x}{2x}$ for $x\\neq0$ and $f(0)=k$ is continuous at $x=0$, find $k$.","image":"","options":["5","1","2.5","0.4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Condition: continuity at 0 needs $k=\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\sin5x}{2x}$.\nRewrite: $\\frac{\\sin5x}{2x}=\\frac52\\cdot\\frac{\\sin5x}{5x}$.\nSince $\\lim_{t\\to0}\\frac{\\sin t}{t}=1$, the limit equals $\\frac52$.\nSo $k=2.5$. Option (4) inverts the factor to $\\frac25$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":123,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (function) with List-II (derivative with respect to $x$).\nList-I:\n(A) $\\cos(\\sin x)$\n(B) $\\sin(x^3)$\n(C) $\\sin\\sqrt{x}$, $x>0$\n(D) $\\tan(2x+1)$\nList-II:\n(I) $3x^2\\cos(x^3)$\n(II) $-\\sin(\\sin x)\\cos x$\n(III) $2\\sec^2(2x+1)$\n(IV) $\\frac{\\cos\\sqrt{x}}{2\\sqrt{x}}$","image":"","options":["A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) With $t = \\sin x$: $\\frac{d}{dx}\\cos t = -\\sin t \\cdot \\cos x = -\\sin(\\sin x)\\cos x$, so A-II.\n(B) With $t = x^3$: $\\cos t \\cdot 3x^2 = 3x^2\\cos(x^3)$, so B-I.\n(C) With $t = \\sqrt{x}$: $\\cos t \\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} = \\frac{\\cos\\sqrt{x}}{2\\sqrt{x}}$, so C-IV.\n(D) With $t = 2x+1$: $\\sec^2 t \\cdot 2 = 2\\sec^2(2x+1)$, so D-III.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":124,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"The number of points in the open interval $(0, 2\\pi)$ at which the function $f(x) = [\\cos x]$ is discontinuous is ($[t]$ = greatest integer $\\le t$):","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"[t] is continuous except at integers. In $(0, 2\\pi)$, cos x takes integer values only at $x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$, where cos x = 0, -1, 0.\nAt $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$: the value is 0 on the left (cos x > 0) and -1 on the right (cos x < 0), so f is discontinuous.\nAt $\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$: the value is -1 on the left and 0 on the right, so f is discontinuous.\nAt $\\pi$: cos x stays in $[-1, -\\frac{1}{2})$ nearby, so f = -1 on both sides and at $\\pi$. So f is continuous there.\nCount = 2.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":125,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following functions are differentiable at $x = 0$?\n$|x|$, $x|x|$, $\\sin x$, $|\\sin x|$, $|x|^3$, $[x]$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Check each function at 0:\n- $|x|$: the left hand derivative is $-1$ and the right hand derivative is $1$. Not differentiable.\n- $x|x|$: $\\frac{h|h|}{h} = |h| \\to 0$ from both sides. Differentiable.\n- $\\sin x$: differentiable, with derivative $\\cos 0 = 1$.\n- $|\\sin x|$: $\\frac{|\\sin h|}{h} \\to -1$ from the left and $1$ from the right. Not differentiable.\n- $|x|^3$: $\\frac{|h|^3}{h} \\to 0$ from both sides. Differentiable.\n- $[x]$: discontinuous at 0, so not differentiable.\nThe count is 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":126,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"A graph shows a function defined on the whole real line. It is a horizontal ray at height 1 for all $x \\le 0$, including a solid dot at $(0, 1)$. It is a horizontal ray at height 2 for all $x > 0$, with an open circle at $(0, 2)$. Which statement about this function at $x = 0$ is correct?","image":"","options":["LHL = 1, RHL = 2, $f(0) = 1$, so the function is discontinuous at 0","LHL = 1, RHL = 2, $f(0) = 2$, so the function is discontinuous at 0","LHL = RHL = 1 but $f(0) = 2$, so the function is discontinuous at 0","The function is continuous at 0 because $f(0)$ equals the left hand limit"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The solid dot at $(0,1)$ gives $f(0) = 1$.\nPoints just left of 0 have value 1, so LHL = 1. Points just right of 0 have value 2, so RHL = 2.\nSince LHL is not equal to RHL, the limit does not exist and $f$ is discontinuous at 0. The pen must be lifted there.\nOption (4) fails because matching only one side is not enough for continuity.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":127,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x) = \\sqrt{\\tan(x^2)}$ for $0 < x < \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}$. The value of $f'(x)$ at $x = \\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi}}{2}$ is","image":"","options":["$\\sqrt{\\pi}$","$2\\sqrt{\\pi}$","$\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi}}{2}$","$\\sqrt{2\\pi}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $f$ is a composite of three functions, with $t = x^2$, $s = \\tan t$ and $f = \\sqrt{s}$.\nChain rule: $f'(x) = \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{s}}\\cdot\\sec^2 t\\cdot 2x = \\frac{x\\sec^2(x^2)}{\\sqrt{\\tan(x^2)}}$.\nAt $x = \\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi}}{2}$: $x^2 = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$, so $\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{4} = 1$ and $\\sec^2\\frac{\\pi}{4} = 2$.\nSubstituting: $f' = \\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi}}{2}\\cdot\\frac{2}{1} = \\sqrt{\\pi}$.\nThe option $2\\sqrt{\\pi}$ comes from dropping the factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ of the square root.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":128,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"The function $f(x)=\\begin{cases} 3, & x\\le 1 \\\\ ax+b, & 1<x<5 \\\\ 15, & x\\ge 5 \\end{cases}$ is continuous everywhere. Then $(a,b)$ equals","image":"","options":["$(3,0)$","$(0,3)$","$(4,-1)$","$(3,3)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"At $x=1$: $f(1)=3$ and RHL $=a+b$. So $a+b=3$.\nAt $x=5$: LHL $=5a+b$ and $f(5)=15$. So $5a+b=15$.\nSubtracting the two equations: $4a=12$, so $a=3$ and $b=0$.\nThe pair $(4,-1)$ satisfies only the first condition, since $5(4)-1=19\\ne 15$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":129,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Let $h(x) = [x] \\sin(\\pi x)$, where $[x]$ denotes the greatest integer less than or equal to x. Which of the following is correct?","image":"","options":["h is discontinuous at every integer, because [x] is discontinuous there","h is discontinuous only at x = 0","h is continuous at every real number","h is continuous only at non-integer points and at x = 0"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"At a non-integer c, both [x] and $\\sin \\pi x$ are continuous, so their product is continuous.\nAt an integer n: $h(n) = n \\sin(n\\pi) = 0$.\nLeft-hand limit $= (n-1)\\sin(n\\pi) = 0$, and right-hand limit $= n \\sin(n\\pi) = 0$.\nAll three values are equal, so h is continuous at n.\nTheorem 1 gives only a sufficient condition. A product can be continuous even when one factor is not, so option (1) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":130,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"The derivative of $f(x) = \\sin^{-1} x$ at $x = \\frac{1}{2}$ is:","image":"","options":["$\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$","$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$","$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$","$2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Formula: for $x \\in (-1, 1)$, $\\frac{d}{dx}\\sin^{-1}x = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$.\nSubstituting $x = \\frac{1}{2}$: $\\sqrt{1 - \\frac{1}{4}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\nSo $f'(\\frac{1}{2}) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}/2} = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$.\nThe option $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is the value of $\\cos y$, not its reciprocal.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":131,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"The point (1, 2) lies on the curve $x^2 + xy + y^2 = 7$. The value of $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at this point is","image":"","options":["$0.8$","$-1.25$","$-0.5$","$-0.8$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $x^2 + xy + y^2 = 7$ at (1, 2). Check: $1 + 2 + 4 = 7$, so the point is on the curve.\nFormula: differentiate implicitly, $2x + y + x\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0$, which gives $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{2x + y}{x + 2y}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{2 + 2}{1 + 4} = -\\frac{4}{5}$.\nSo $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -0.8$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":132,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Let $y=\\sqrt{\\cos(x^2)}$, defined near $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}$. The value of $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}$ is","image":"","options":["$-\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}$","$\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}$","$-\\sqrt{2\\pi}$","$-\\frac{\\sqrt{3\\pi}}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: three layers, $s=x^2$, $t=\\cos s$, $y=\\sqrt{t}$.\nFormula: $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\cdot(-\\sin s)\\cdot 2x=-\\frac{x\\sin(x^2)}{\\sqrt{\\cos(x^2)}}$.\nAt $x^2=\\frac{\\pi}{3}$: $\\sin=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\sqrt{\\cos}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}\\cdot\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot\\sqrt{2}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}{2}$.\nSo $\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}$. Option 3 misses the $\\frac{1}{2}$ from the derivative of the square root.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":133,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $y = e^{m\\sin^{-1}x}$ for $-1 < x < 1$, where m is a constant, which of the following differential equations does y satisfy?","image":"","options":["$(1 - x^2)y_2 - xy_1 - m^2y = 0$","$(1 - x^2)y_2 - xy_1 + m^2y = 0$","$(1 - x^2)y_2 + xy_1 - m^2y = 0$","$(1 + x^2)y_2 - xy_1 - m^2y = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"First derivative: $y_1 = \\frac{my}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$, so $(1-x^2)y_1^2 = m^2y^2$.\nDifferentiate: $(1-x^2)\\cdot 2y_1y_2 - 2xy_1^2 = 2m^2yy_1$.\nDivide by $2y_1$ (which is nonzero): $(1-x^2)y_2 - xy_1 = m^2y$.\nThis rearranges to option (1). The sign of the $m^2y$ term is positive on the right side, which rules out option (2).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":134,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x) = (1+x)(1+x^2)(1+x^4)(1+x^8)(1+x^{16})$. The value of $f'(1)$ is","image":"","options":["248","496","31","512"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Take logs: $\\log f = \\log(1+x) + \\log(1+x^2) + \\dots + \\log(1+x^{16})$.\nDifferentiate: $\\frac{f'}{f} = \\frac{1}{1+x} + \\frac{2x}{1+x^2} + \\frac{4x^3}{1+x^4} + \\frac{8x^7}{1+x^8} + \\frac{16x^{15}}{1+x^{16}}$.\nAt $x = 1$ this sum is $\\frac{1+2+4+8+16}{2} = 15.5$.\nAlso $f(1) = 2^5 = 32$.\nSo $f'(1) = 32 \\times 15.5 = 496$.\nOption 248 comes from forgetting that $f(1) = 32$, not 16.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":135,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is correct for a real function $f$ and a point $c$ in its domain?","image":"","options":["If $f$ is continuous at $c$, then $f$ is differentiable at $c$","If $f$ is differentiable at $c$, then $\\lim_{x \\to c} f(x) = f(c)$","If $f$ is not differentiable at $c$, then $f$ is discontinuous at $c$","A function differentiable at $c$ may have finite but unequal LHD and RHD at $c$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: $|x|$ is continuous at 0 but not differentiable there.\n(2) Correct: by Theorem 3, differentiability at $c$ implies continuity at $c$, i.e. $\\lim_{x \\to c} f(x) = f(c)$.\n(3) Incorrect: this is the false converse again. $|x|$ is not differentiable at 0 yet is continuous there.\n(4) Incorrect: differentiability requires the LHD and RHD to be finite and equal.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":136,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"In which of the following relations is y given implicitly as a function of x, so that it cannot easily be rearranged into the form $y = f(x)$?","image":"","options":["$3x - 2y = 7$","$y = x^2 + \\sin x$","$y - e^x = 4$","$x + \\sin(xy) - y = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"If we can easily solve for y and write $y = f(x)$, y is an explicit function of x. Otherwise the relation gives y implicitly.\n(1) $3x - 2y = 7$ rearranges to $y = \\frac{3x-7}{2}$, so it is explicit.\n(2) $y = x^2 + \\sin x$ is already explicit.\n(3) $y - e^x = 4$ rearranges to $y = e^x + 4$, so it is explicit.\n(4) In $x + \\sin(xy) - y = 0$, y appears both inside the sine and outside it, so there is no easy way to isolate y. This is NCERT's example of an implicit relation.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":137,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For the curve $x^{\\frac{2}{3}} + y^{\\frac{2}{3}} = a^{\\frac{2}{3}}$ (with $x, y \\neq 0$), $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{x}}$.\nReason (R): Taking $x = a\\cos^3\\theta$, $y = a\\sin^3\\theta$ satisfies the curve and gives $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\tan\\theta$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: $(a\\cos^3\\theta)^{2/3} + (a\\sin^3\\theta)^{2/3} = a^{2/3}(\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta) = a^{2/3}$, so the parametrisation fits the curve.\nFor the slope: $\\frac{dy}{d\\theta} = 3a\\sin^2\\theta\\cos\\theta$ and $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta} = -3a\\cos^2\\theta\\sin\\theta$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\tan\\theta$.\nA is true: $\\frac{y}{x} = \\tan^3\\theta$, so $-\\tan\\theta = -\\sqrt[3]{y/x}$.\nA follows directly from R.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":138,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (function) with List-II (differential equation satisfied). Here A and B are arbitrary constants.\nList-I:\n(A) $y = 2\\cos(\\log x) - 5\\sin(\\log x)$, $x > 0$\n(B) $y = 4e^{2x} - e^{3x}$\n(C) $y = A\\sin x + B\\cos x$\n(D) $y = Ae^{x} + Be^{-x}$\nList-II:\n(I) $y_2 - y = 0$\n(II) $x^2y_2 + xy_1 + y = 0$\n(III) $y_2 + y = 0$\n(IV) $y_2 - 5y_1 + 6y = 0$","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $xy_1 = -2\\sin(\\log x) - 5\\cos(\\log x)$. Differentiating gives $xy_2 + y_1 = -\\frac{y}{x}$, so $x^2y_2 + xy_1 + y = 0$. This is II.\n(B) The exponents 2 and 3 are the roots of $r^2 - 5r + 6 = 0$, so $y_2 - 5y_1 + 6y = 0$. This is IV.\n(C) $y_2 = -A\\sin x - B\\cos x = -y$, so $y_2 + y = 0$. This is III.\n(D) $y_2 = Ae^x + Be^{-x} = y$, so $y_2 - y = 0$. This is I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":139,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"The number of points of discontinuity of the function $f(x) = |x| - |x+1|$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, is:","image":"","options":["0","1","2","3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The modulus function is continuous everywhere.\nThe function x + 1 is a polynomial, so $|x+1|$ is continuous everywhere, being a composite of continuous functions.\nThe difference of two continuous functions is continuous, so f is continuous at every real x.\nAt x = 0 and x = -1 the graph has corners (f is not differentiable there), but there are no breaks.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":140,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"The point $(1, 2)$ lies on the curve $x^2 + xy + y^2 = 7$. What is the value of $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at this point?","image":"","options":["$-\\frac{4}{5}$","$-\\frac{5}{4}$","$\\frac{4}{5}$","$-\\frac{1}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Check the point: $1 + 2 + 4 = 7$, so $(1, 2)$ lies on the curve.\nDifferentiate implicitly w.r.t. x, using the product rule on $xy$: $2x + (y + x\\frac{dy}{dx}) + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0$.\nCollect the derivative terms: $\\frac{dy}{dx}(x + 2y) = -(2x + y)$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{2x + y}{x + 2y}$.\nSubstitute $(1, 2)$: $-\\frac{2 + 2}{1 + 4} = -\\frac{4}{5}$.\nThe value $-\\frac{5}{4}$ comes from writing the fraction upside down.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":141,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (functions) with List-II (behaviour at $x = 0$).\nList-I:\n(A) $|x|$\n(B) $x|x|$\n(C) $[x]$\n(D) $|x|^3$\nList-II:\n(i) LHD $= -1$ and RHD $= 1$\n(ii) differentiable, but the second derivative does not exist\n(iii) not continuous\n(iv) second derivative exists","image":"","options":["A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $|x|$: the one-sided difference quotients are $\\frac{-h}{h} = -1$ and $\\frac{h}{h} = 1$. So A matches (i).\n(B) $x|x|$: $f'(x) = 2|x|$, but $2|x|$ has a corner at 0, so $f''(0)$ does not exist. B matches (ii).\n(C) $[x]$: the LHL at 0 is $-1$ while $f(0) = 0$, so $[x]$ is discontinuous at 0. C matches (iii).\n(D) $|x|^3$: $f''(x) = 6|x|$, so $f''(0) = 0$ exists. D matches (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":142,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{\\sin x}{x}-2[x], & x<0\\\\ a, & x=0\\\\ b+x^2\\sin\\frac{1}{x}, & x>0\\end{cases}$, where $[x]$ is the greatest integer function. If f is continuous at $x=0$, then $a+b$ equals:","image":"","options":["6","2","-2","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"LHL: as $x\\to0^-$, $\\frac{\\sin x}{x}\\to1$ and $[x]=-1$.\nSo LHL = $1-2(-1)=3$.\nRHL: since $|x^2\\sin\\frac1x|\\le x^2\\to0$, RHL = b.\nContinuity requires LHL = RHL = f(0), so b = 3 and a = 3.\nHence a + b = 6.\nThe tempting value 2 comes from wrongly taking [x] = 0 for x slightly less than 0.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":143,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The function $\\sin^{-1}x$ is not differentiable at $x = 1$.\nReason (R): On differentiating $x = \\sin y$, we get $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{1}{\\cos y}$, which is defined only when $\\cos y \\neq 0$, i.e., $x \\neq \\pm 1$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let $y = \\sin^{-1}x$, so $x = \\sin y$.\nDifferentiating gives $1 = \\cos y \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{1}{\\cos y}$.\nAt $x = 1$, $y = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\cos y = 0$. The derivative blows up, so it does not exist as a real number. Hence A is true.\nR states exactly this condition, $\\cos y \\neq 0 \\Leftrightarrow x \\in (-1, 1)$. So R is true and it explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":144,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $y=\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3x-x^3}{1-3x^2}\\right)$, where $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}<x<\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$, then $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ is:","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{1+x^2}$","$-\\frac{3}{1+x^2}$","$\\frac{3}{1+x^2}$","$\\frac{3}{1-x^2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Substitute $x=\\tan\\theta$. Then $\\frac{3\\tan\\theta-\\tan^3\\theta}{1-3\\tan^2\\theta}=\\tan3\\theta$.\nFor $|x|<\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$, $\\theta\\in\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{6},\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)$, so $3\\theta\\in\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$.\nHence $y=\\tan^{-1}(\\tan3\\theta)=3\\theta=3\\tan^{-1}x$.\nSo $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3}{1+x^2}$.\nThe option $\\frac{1}{1+x^2}$ ignores the factor 3 coming from the triple angle.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":145,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The function $f(x) = x|x|$ is differentiable at $x = 0$.\nStatement II: The function $g(x) = |x|$ is a differentiable function on $\\mathbb{R}$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: $f'(0) = \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{h|h| - 0}{h} = \\lim_{h \\to 0} |h| = 0$. The limit exists from both sides, so $f$ is differentiable at 0. Statement I is true.\nStatement II: at $x = 0$, the LHD of $|x|$ is $-1$ and the RHD is $1$. So $|x|$ is not differentiable at 0, and hence it is not a differentiable function on $\\mathbb{R}$. Statement II is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":146,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The function $g(x) = x - [x]$, where $[x]$ is the greatest integer function, is discontinuous at every integer.\nStatement II: If $f + g$ is continuous at $x = c$, then both $f$ and $g$ must be continuous at $x = c$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: take an integer $c$. Then $\\lim_{x\\to c^-}(x - [x]) = c - (c - 1) = 1$, while $\\lim_{x\\to c^+}(x - [x]) = c - c = 0$. The one-sided limits differ, so $g$ is discontinuous at every integer. True.\nStatement II: Theorem 1 says continuity of $f$ and $g$ implies continuity of $f + g$. The converse fails. Take $f(x) = [x]$ and $g(x) = -[x]$. Both are discontinuous at integers, but $f + g = 0$ is continuous everywhere. False.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":147,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (function) with List-II (derivative), in the appropriate domains.\nList-I:\n(a) $\\sin^{-1} x$\n(b) $\\tan^{-1} x$\n(c) $e^x$\n(d) $\\log x$\nList-II:\n(i) $\\frac{1}{x}$\n(ii) $\\frac{1}{1+x^2}$\n(iii) $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$\n(iv) $e^x$","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-ii, c-iv, d-i","a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i","a-iii, b-ii, c-i, d-iv","a-i, b-ii, c-iv, d-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) $\\frac{d}{dx}\\sin^{-1}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$ for $x\\in(-1,1)$, so a-iii.\n(b) $\\frac{d}{dx}\\tan^{-1}x=\\frac{1}{1+x^2}$ for all real $x$, so b-ii.\n(c) $e^x$ is unchanged on differentiation, so c-iv.\n(d) $\\frac{d}{dx}\\log x=\\frac{1}{x}$ for $x>0$, so d-i.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":148,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Match each function in List-I with its behaviour at $x=0$ in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) $f(x)=\\frac{|x|}{x}$ for $x\\neq0$, $f(0)=0$\n(B) $f(x)=\\frac{\\sin x}{x}$ for $x\\neq0$, $f(0)=2$\n(C) $f(x)=x^2\\sin\\frac{1}{x}$ for $x\\neq0$, $f(0)=0$\n(D) $f(x)=x-[x]$\nList-II\n(i) Continuous at 0\n(ii) Limit exists but differs from $f(0)$\n(iii) LHL $=-1$, RHL $=1$\n(iv) RHL equals $f(0)$ but LHL does not","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-iii, C-i, D-iv","A-iii, B-ii, C-iv, D-i","A-iv, B-ii, C-i, D-iii","A-iii, B-ii, C-i, D-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) For $x<0$, $f=-1$; for $x>0$, $f=1$. So LHL $=-1$ and RHL $=1$, giving (iii).\n(B) $\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\sin x}{x}=1$, but $f(0)=2$. This gives (ii).\n(C) $|x^2\\sin\\frac1x|\\le x^2\\to0$, so the limit is $0=f(0)$. This gives (i).\n(D) LHL $=0-(-1)=1$, while RHL $=0=f(0)$. This gives (iv).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":149,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos^{-1} x) = -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$, and this formula holds for $x \\in (-1, 1)$.\nStatement II: $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\tan^{-1} x) = \\frac{1}{1+x^2}$, and this formula holds for every real $x$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The derivative of $\\cos^{-1}x$ is $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$. It requires $1 - x^2 > 0$, so it holds only on $(-1, 1)$.\nStatement II is correct. $1 + x^2 > 0$ for every real $x$, so $\\frac{1}{1+x^2}$ is defined on all of R.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":150,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $x = e^{\\log x}$ holds for every real number x.\nReason (R): The domain of the logarithm function is the set of positive real numbers.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"R is true: logarithm is not defined for non-positive numbers, so the domain is $R^+$.\nBecause of this, $e^{\\log x}$ is meaningful only when x > 0.\nFor x > 0, put $y = e^{\\log x}$; then $\\log y = \\log x$, so y = x.\nFor x ≤ 0, the right side is undefined, so the identity fails.\nHence A is false and R is true.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":151,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x)=(x^2-1)[x]$ be defined on the closed interval $[-2, 2]$, where $[x]$ is the greatest integer function. How many points of discontinuity does $f$ have on $[-2,2]$?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Only integers can be problem points. At an interior integer $n$: LHL $=(n^2-1)(n-1)$ and RHL $=f(n)=(n^2-1)n$.\nThese are equal only when $n^2-1=0$.\n$n=\\pm1$: continuous. $n=0$: LHL $=1$, RHL $=0$, so discontinuous.\nEndpoint $-2$ needs only the RHL: RHL $=3(-2)=-6=f(-2)$, so continuous.\nEndpoint $2$ needs only the LHL: LHL $=3\\cdot1=3$ but $f(2)=6$, so discontinuous.\nSo the discontinuities are at $0$ and $2$, giving 2 points.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":152,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $y = x^{\\sin x}$ for $x>0$, the value of $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at $x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ is:","image":"","options":["$1$","$0$","$\\frac{\\pi}{2}$","$\\frac{2}{\\pi}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$, so $\\sin x = 1$, $\\cos x=0$ and $y = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\nFormula: $\\log y = \\sin x\\log x$ gives $\\frac{dy}{dx} = y\\left(\\frac{\\sin x}{x}+\\cos x\\log x\\right)$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\pi/2}+0\\right)$.\nSo $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 1$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":153,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"The number of points in the open interval $(0, 5)$ at which the greatest integer function $f(x)=[x]$ is discontinuous is:","image":"","options":["4","5","3","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact: [x] is discontinuous exactly at integers.\nAt an integer c, LHL = c − 1 and RHL = c, so the two limits differ.\nAt non-integer points the function is locally constant, so it is continuous there.\nThe integers inside (0, 5) are 1, 2, 3 and 4. The endpoints 0 and 5 are excluded.\nSo there are 4 points of discontinuity.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":154,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Using the change of base rule, the value of $\\log_8 32$ is:","image":"","options":["$\\frac{4}{3}$","$\\frac{5}{3}$","$\\frac{3}{5}$","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Formula: $\\log_a p = \\frac{\\log_b p}{\\log_b a}$.\nTake b = 2: $\\log_8 32 = \\frac{\\log_2 32}{\\log_2 8}$.\nSince $2^5 = 32$ and $2^3 = 8$, this equals $\\frac{5}{3}$.\nOption (3) inverts the ratio by mistake.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":155,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following functions are continuous functions (that is, continuous at every point of their own domain)?\n$x^3 + x^2 - 1$, $\\frac{1}{x}$, $\\tan x$, $[x]$, $|x|$, $\\sin(x^2)$","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Check each function:\n- $x^3 + x^2 - 1$ is a polynomial, so it is continuous everywhere.\n- $\\frac{1}{x}$ is continuous at every point of its domain $x \\neq 0$, so it is a continuous function.\n- $\\tan x$ is a quotient of continuous functions and is continuous wherever it is defined.\n- $[x]$ jumps at every integer, so it is not continuous.\n- $|x|$ is continuous everywhere.\n- $\\sin(x^2)$ is a composite of continuous functions, so it is continuous.\nHence 5 of the 6 are continuous.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":156,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $f(x) = (3x-2)^5$, then the value of $f'(1)$ is","image":"","options":["15","5","3","45"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $f(x)=(3x-2)^5$, a composite with inner $t=3x-2$ and outer $t^5$.\nFormula (chain rule): $f'(x)=5t^4\\cdot\\frac{dt}{dx}=5(3x-2)^4\\cdot 3=15(3x-2)^4$.\nSubstituting $x=1$: $3(1)-2=1$, so $f'(1)=15(1)^4$.\nSo $f'(1)=15$. Option 5 comes from forgetting the inner factor 3.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":157,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For any constants A and B, the function $y = A\\cos 2x + B\\sin 2x$ satisfies $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} + 4y = 0$.\nReason (R): For any constant k, $\\frac{d^2}{dx^2}(\\cos kx) = -k^2\\cos kx$ and $\\frac{d^2}{dx^2}(\\sin kx) = -k^2\\sin kx$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R: $\\frac{d}{dx}\\cos kx = -k\\sin kx$, so $\\frac{d^2}{dx^2}\\cos kx = -k^2\\cos kx$. The same working applies to $\\sin kx$. R is true.\nA: Using R with $k = 2$, $y'' = -4A\\cos 2x - 4B\\sin 2x = -4y$, so $y'' + 4y = 0$. A is true.\nA follows directly from R.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":158,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"The function $f(x)=\\begin{cases} 4, & x\\le 1 \\\\ ax^2+b, & 1<x<3 \\\\ 20, & x\\ge 3 \\end{cases}$ is continuous on $\\mathbb{R}$. The value of $f(2)$ is","image":"","options":["10","8","12","14"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Continuity at $x=1$: $a+b=4$.\nContinuity at $x=3$: $9a+b=20$.\nSubtracting: $8a=16$, so $a=2$ and then $b=2$.\nSince $1<2<3$, $f(2)=a(2)^2+b=8+2$.\nSo $f(2)=10$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":159,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Let $\\cos y = x \\cos(a + y)$, where a is a constant with $\\sin a \\neq 0$. At the point of the curve where x = 0 and $y = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, the value of $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ is","image":"","options":["$\\cos a$","$\\sin a$","$-\\sin a$","$\\frac{1}{\\sin a}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"First write x explicitly in terms of y: $x = \\frac{\\cos y}{\\cos(a+y)}$.\nDifferentiate with respect to y: $\\frac{dx}{dy} = \\frac{-\\sin y \\cos(a+y) + \\cos y \\sin(a+y)}{\\cos^2(a+y)}$.\nThe numerator is $\\sin((a+y) - y) = \\sin a$, so $\\frac{dx}{dy} = \\frac{\\sin a}{\\cos^2(a+y)}$. Hence $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{\\cos^2(a+y)}{\\sin a}$.\nAt $y = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$: $\\cos(a + \\frac{\\pi}{2}) = -\\sin a$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{\\sin^2 a}{\\sin a} = \\sin a$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":160,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Let f be a real function defined only on the closed interval $[a, b]$. For f to be continuous at the left end point a, the condition required is:","image":"","options":["$\\lim_{x\\to a^-} f(x)=f(a)$","Both $\\lim_{x\\to a^-} f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to a^+} f(x)$ equal $f(a)$","$f(a)=f(b)$","$\\lim_{x\\to a^+} f(x)=f(a)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The function is not defined to the left of a.\nSo $\\lim_{x\\to a^-}f(x)$ makes no sense, and options (1) and (2) fail.\nNCERT defines continuity at the end point a as $\\lim_{x\\to a^+}f(x)=f(a)$. Similarly, continuity at b means $\\lim_{x\\to b^-}f(x)=f(b)$.\nThe condition f(a) = f(b) has nothing to do with continuity.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":161,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (parametric equations) with List-II ($\\frac{dy}{dx}$):\nList-I\n(A) $x = 2\\sec\\theta$, $y = 3\\tan\\theta$\n(B) $x = \\cos t$, $y = \\cos 2t$\n(C) $x = 2t$, $y = \\frac{8}{t}$\n(D) $x = e^t$, $y = e^{-t}$\nList-II\n(i) $-\\frac{4}{t^2}$\n(ii) $4\\cos t$\n(iii) $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cosec}\\,\\theta$\n(iv) $-e^{-2t}$","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{3\\sec^2\\theta}{2\\sec\\theta\\tan\\theta} = \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\sin\\theta} = \\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cosec}\\,\\theta$, which is (iii).\n(B) $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{-2\\sin 2t}{-\\sin t} = \\frac{4\\sin t\\cos t}{\\sin t} = 4\\cos t$, which is (ii).\n(C) $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{-8/t^2}{2} = -\\frac{4}{t^2}$, which is (i).\n(D) $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{-e^{-t}}{e^t} = -e^{-2t}$, which is (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":162,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $f(x) = (1+x)(1+x^2)(1+x^4)$, then $f'(1)$ equals:","image":"","options":["7","14","28","56"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $f(1) = 2\\cdot2\\cdot2 = 8$.\nFormula: $\\log f = \\log(1+x)+\\log(1+x^2)+\\log(1+x^4)$, so $\\frac{f'}{f} = \\frac{1}{1+x}+\\frac{2x}{1+x^2}+\\frac{4x^3}{1+x^4}$.\nSubstituting x = 1: $\\frac{f'(1)}{8} = \\frac12+1+2 = 3.5$.\nSo $f'(1) = 8\\times3.5 = 28$.\nCheck: $f(x) = 1+x+\\dots+x^7$, so $f'(1) = 1+2+\\dots+7 = 28$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":163,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"How many points of $\\mathbb{R}$ are there at which $f(x) = |x| + |x - 1| + |x - 2|$ is NOT differentiable?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","0"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Each modulus term has a corner at its zero: $x = 0$, $x = 1$ and $x = 2$.\nAway from these three points, $f$ is linear, so it is differentiable there.\nAt $x = 1$: the slope just to the left is $1 - 1 - 1 = -1$ and the slope just to the right is $1 + 1 - 1 = 1$. These differ.\nSimilarly, at $x = 0$ the slope jumps from $-3$ to $-1$, and at $x = 2$ it jumps from $1$ to $3$.\nSo $f$ is not differentiable at 3 points.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":164,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"The derivative of $e^{-x}$ with respect to x is:","image":"","options":["$e^{-x}$","$-e^{-x}$","$-x\\,e^{-x-1}$","$-e^{x}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Let $y = e^{t}$ with $t = -x$.\nChain rule: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = e^{t}\\cdot\\frac{dt}{dx} = e^{-x}\\cdot(-1) = -e^{-x}$.\nOption (1) leaves out the factor $\\frac{d}{dx}(-x) = -1$.\nOption (3) wrongly applies the power rule $nx^{n-1}$ to an exponential.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":165,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"If $y + \\cos y = \\sin x$, then $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ equals","image":"","options":["$\\frac{\\cos x}{1 + \\sin y}$","$\\frac{\\cos x}{1 - \\sin y}$","$-\\frac{\\cos x}{1 - \\sin y}$","$\\frac{\\cos x}{1 + \\cos y}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Differentiate both sides with respect to x, treating y as a function of x.\nBy the chain rule, $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos y) = -\\sin y \\frac{dy}{dx}$. So $\\frac{dy}{dx} - \\sin y \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\cos x$.\nTaking out the common factor: $\\frac{dy}{dx}(1 - \\sin y) = \\cos x$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{\\cos x}{1 - \\sin y}$.\nOption (1) comes from forgetting the minus sign in the derivative of $\\cos y$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":166,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Theorem 2 of the chapter deals with continuity of composite functions. Suppose f and g are real functions such that $f \\circ g$ is defined at c, and g is continuous at c. Then $f \\circ g$ is continuous at c if f is continuous at:","image":"","options":["c","f(c)","g(c)","every real number"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Near x = c, the inner function sends x to values close to g(c).\nSo the outer function f has to behave well near the point g(c), not near c.\nTheorem 2: if g is continuous at c and f is continuous at g(c), then $f \\circ g$ is continuous at c.\nRequiring f to be continuous everywhere would be sufficient but is not what the theorem needs.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":167,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"A point moves on the circle $x = 5\\cos\\theta$, $y = 5\\sin\\theta$. What is the value of $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$?","image":"","options":["$-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$","$-\\sqrt{3}$","$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$","$\\sqrt{3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $x = 5\\cos\\theta$, $y = 5\\sin\\theta$.\nFormula: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{dy/d\\theta}{dx/d\\theta} = \\frac{5\\cos\\theta}{-5\\sin\\theta} = -\\cot\\theta$.\nSubstituting $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$: $-\\cot\\frac{\\pi}{3} = -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$.\nIf you get $-\\sqrt{3}$, you have used $-\\tan\\theta$ by mistake.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":168,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Consider $f(x) = |x^2 - 3x + 2|$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Which of the following is correct?","image":"","options":["$f$ is continuous everywhere and fails to be differentiable at exactly two points, $x = 1$ and $x = 2$","At $x = 1$, the LHD of $f$ is $1$ and the RHD is $-1$","$f$ is not differentiable at $x = \\frac{3}{2}$, because $f$ has a local maximum there","$f$ is discontinuous at $x = 1$ and $x = 2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let $g = (x-1)(x-2)$, so $g' = 2x - 3$. Then $f = |g|$ is a composite of continuous functions, hence continuous everywhere. This makes (4) false.\nAt $x = 1$: for $x < 1$, $g > 0$, so the LHD is $g'(1) = -1$. For $1 < x < 2$, $g < 0$, so the RHD is $-g'(1) = 1$. The values are unequal, and (2) has them reversed.\nAt $x = 2$: the LHD is $-g'(2) = -1$ and the RHD is $g'(2) = 1$, so $f$ is not differentiable there.\nAt $x = \\frac{3}{2}$: $f = -g$ is smooth, with $f'(\\frac{3}{2}) = -(2 \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} - 3) = 0$. So $f$ is differentiable there and (3) is false.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-169","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":169,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos^{-1}x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$ for $x\\in(-1,1)$","The derivative of $\\sin^{-1}x$ exists at $x=1$","$\\frac{d}{dx}(\\tan^{-1}x)=\\frac{1}{1+x^2}$ for every real $x$","$\\frac{d}{dx}(\\tan^{-1}x)=\\frac{1}{1-x^2}$ for $x\\in(-1,1)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: the derivative of $\\cos^{-1}x$ is $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$. The sign is negative.\n(2) Incorrect: $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$ is undefined at $x=\\pm1$, so the formula holds only on $(-1,1)$.\n(3) Correct: $1+x^2>0$ for every real $x$, so the derivative of $\\tan^{-1}x$ exists on all of R.\n(4) Incorrect: the denominator is $1+x^2$, not $1-x^2$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-170","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":170,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Each function in List-I can be written as $f \\circ g$ for one pair (f, g) in List-II. Match them.\nList-I:\n(a) $\\sin |x|$\n(b) $|\\cos x|$\n(c) $\\cos (x^2)$\n(d) $|1 - x + |x||$\nList-II:\n(P) $f(x) = |x|,\\ g(x) = \\cos x$\n(Q) $f(x) = \\sin x,\\ g(x) = |x|$\n(R) $f(x) = \\cos x,\\ g(x) = x^2$\n(S) $f(x) = |x|,\\ g(x) = 1 - x + |x|$","image":"","options":["a-Q, b-P, c-R, d-S","a-P, b-Q, c-R, d-S","a-Q, b-P, c-S, d-R","a-P, b-Q, c-S, d-R"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In $f \\circ g$, the function g acts first (inside) and f acts last (outside).\n(a) $\\sin|x|$: inside $|x|$, outside sin, so Q.\n(b) $|\\cos x|$: inside cos x, outside $|\\cdot|$, so P. Pair Q would instead give $\\sin|x|$.\n(c) $\\cos(x^2)$: inside $x^2$, outside cos, so R.\n(d) inside $1 - x + |x|$, outside $|\\cdot|$, so S.\nEvery inner and outer function is continuous, so all four composites are continuous.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-171","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":171,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If $y = (\\sin^{-1}x)^2$ for $-1 < x < 1$, then $(1-x^2)\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} - x\\frac{dy}{dx} = 2$.\nStatement II: For $x > 0$, $\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(x^{1/x}\\right) = x^{1/x}\\cdot\\frac{1 - \\log x}{x^2}$.\n\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: $y_1 = \\frac{2\\sin^{-1}x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$, so $(1-x^2)y_1^2 = 4(\\sin^{-1}x)^2 = 4y$.\nDifferentiating gives $(1-x^2)\\cdot 2y_1y_2 - 2xy_1^2 = 4y_1$.\nDividing by $2y_1$ gives $(1-x^2)y_2 - xy_1 = 2$. So Statement I is true.\nStatement II: let $y = x^{1/x}$, so $\\log y = \\frac{\\log x}{x}$.\nThen $\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{x\\cdot\\frac{1}{x} - \\log x}{x^2} = \\frac{1 - \\log x}{x^2}$. So Statement II is true.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-172","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":172,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements, where y is defined implicitly as a function of x.\nStatement I: If $3x - 2y = \\cos y$, then $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{3}{2 - \\sin y}$ wherever $\\sin y \\neq 2$.\nStatement II: If $\\sin(xy) + x = 1$, then $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{1 + x\\cos(xy)}{y\\cos(xy)}$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: Differentiate w.r.t. x to get $3 - 2\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\sin y\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}$.\nThis gives $3 = \\frac{dy}{dx}(2 - \\sin y)$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{3}{2 - \\sin y}$. Statement I is correct (and $\\sin y \\neq 2$ always holds).\nStatement II: By the chain rule, $\\cos(xy)\\,(y + x\\frac{dy}{dx}) + 1 = 0$.\nSo $x\\cos(xy)\\frac{dy}{dx} = -(1 + y\\cos(xy))$, which gives $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{1 + y\\cos(xy)}{x\\cos(xy)}$.\nThe given expression has x and y interchanged, so Statement II is incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-173","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":173,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"For $x>0$, the derivative of $\\cos\\sqrt{x}$ with respect to $x$ is","image":"","options":["$-\\sin\\sqrt{x}$","$\\frac{\\sin\\sqrt{x}}{2\\sqrt{x}}$","$-\\frac{\\sin\\sqrt{x}}{2\\sqrt{x}}$","$-\\frac{\\sin\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{x}}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Let $t=\\sqrt{x}=x^{1/2}$. Then $\\frac{dt}{dx}=\\frac{1}{2}x^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}$.\nAlso $\\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t)=-\\sin t$.\nChain rule: $\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\sin\\sqrt{x}\\cdot\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}=-\\frac{\\sin\\sqrt{x}}{2\\sqrt{x}}$.\nOption 1 ignores the inner derivative. Option 4 drops the factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ from the power rule.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-174","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":174,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The function $f(x)=\\begin{cases} \\lambda(x^2-4x), & x\\le 0 \\\\ 3x, & x>0 \\end{cases}$ is continuous at $x=0$ for every real $\\lambda$.\nReason (R): For every $\\lambda$, $\\lambda(x^2-4x)$ equals 0 at $x=0$, and $\\lim_{x\\to 0^+}3x=0$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R: $f(0)=\\lambda(0)=0$ for every $\\lambda$, and LHL $=0$ as well. RHL $=\\lim_{x\\to 0^+}3x=0$. So R is true.\nA: Since LHL = RHL = $f(0)=0$, $f$ is continuous at 0 for all $\\lambda$. So A is true.\nR states exactly why the three values agree, so it correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-175","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":175,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $f(x) = |x|$ is continuous at $x = 0$ but not differentiable at $x = 0$.\nReason (R): At $x = 0$, the left hand derivative of $|x|$ is $-1$ and the right hand derivative is $1$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Continuity: both one-sided limits of $|x|$ at 0 equal $0 = f(0)$, so $|x|$ is continuous at 0.\nLHD $= \\lim_{h \\to 0^-} \\frac{-h}{h} = -1$ and RHD $= \\lim_{h \\to 0^+} \\frac{h}{h} = 1$.\nThe one-sided derivatives are unequal, so the derivative does not exist. Hence A is true.\nR is true, and it is exactly the reason why the derivative fails to exist.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-CONTINUITY-176","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Continuity and Differentiability","qno":176,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Continuity and Differentiability","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x) = \\begin{cases} ax^2 + b, & x \\le 1 \\\\ |x-3|, & x > 1 \\end{cases}$\nIf f is differentiable at $x = 1$, and N denotes the number of real points at which f is not differentiable, then the value of $a + b + N$ is:","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Near $x = 1$ from the right, $|x-3| = 3 - x$.\nContinuity at 1: $a + b = 3 - 1 = 2$.\nDifferentiability at 1: LHD $= 2a$ and RHD $= -1$, so $a = -\\frac{1}{2}$ and $b = \\frac{5}{2}$.\nFor $x<1$, f is a polynomial, so it is differentiable there. For $1<x<3$ and $x>3$, f is linear, so it is differentiable there too.\nAt $x = 3$, LHD $= -1$ and RHD $= 1$, which is a corner. So $N = 1$.\nTherefore $a + b + N = 2 + 1 = 3$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 5 · Continuity and Differentiability","difficulty":"hard"}]});
